window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("2timothy", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"2 Timothy","chapters":4,"bigPicture":{"subtitle":"The Last Words of a Faithful Soldier","paragraphs":["Picture an old man in a cold prison cell in Rome. Acts ended with Paul under house arrest, “preaching God’s Kingdom, and teaching the things concerning the Lord Jesus Christ with all boldness, without hindrance” (Acts 28:31). That was years ago. Since then, it seems, he was released, travelled again, wrote 1 Timothy and Titus, and was arrested a second time. Now things are very different. Nero is emperor, Christians are being blamed and hunted, and Paul is chained like a criminal. Most of his friends have left. He has already faced one hearing. He knows the next one will end in death. And he picks up his pen one last time to write to the young man he loves most.","2 Timothy is Paul's final letter — his last will and testament. He writes it knowing that “I am already being offered, and the time of my departure has come” (4:6). It is intensely personal. He remembers Timothy's tears; he longs to see him; he asks him to bring his cloak before winter and his books and parchments. He grieves that “all who are in Asia turned away from me” (1:15) and that “Only Luke is with me” (4:11). Yet there is no self-pity in the letter. It is full of courage, faith and a clear-eyed view of eternity.","The central concern is the gospel's future. Paul is about to die. What will happen to the message he has carried for thirty years? It must be guarded and passed on. So he charges Timothy: guard the good deposit, do not be ashamed, endure suffering, entrust the teaching to faithful people who will teach others, hold to Scripture, preach the word in season and out of season. One generation hands the torch to the next.","And then, near the end, come some of the most famous words Paul ever wrote: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (4:7). Paul is not boasting; he is testifying that God's grace has carried him to the finish. And he looks beyond the executioner to “the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day; and not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing” (4:8). 2 Timothy teaches you how to live faithfully — and how to finish well."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"1 Timothy","intro":"The Holy Spirit placed 2 Timothy directly after 1 Timothy. The first letter told Timothy how to order God's household. The second, written years later from death row, tells him how to keep the faith alive after Paul is gone.","points":[{"title":"From Guarding to Guarding and Handing On","body":"1 Timothy ended with the charge: “Timothy, guard that which is committed to you” (1 Timothy 6:20). 2 Timothy repeats it — “That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” (1:14) — and adds the next step: “The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (2:2). The deposit is not only to be kept safe but passed on to the next generation."},{"title":"From ‘Don't Let Them Despise Your Youth’ to ‘Don't Be Ashamed’","body":"In 1 Timothy Paul wrote, “Let no man despise your youth” (1 Timothy 4:12). Now the danger is not that others will look down on Timothy, but that Timothy will be ashamed — of the gospel, of Paul in chains, of suffering. So Paul writes: “Therefore don’t be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner; but endure hardship for the Good News according to the power of God” (1:8). Courage, not just competence, is now what Timothy needs."},{"title":"From ‘Fight the Good Fight’ to ‘I Have Fought the Good Fight’","body":"1 Timothy charged Timothy to “Fight the good fight of faith” (1 Timothy 6:12). In 2 Timothy, Paul tells Timothy that he himself has done exactly that, to the end: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (4:7). The command becomes a testimony. The old soldier shows the young one that the fight can be finished faithfully."},{"title":"From Order in the Church to Faithfulness in Hard Times","body":"1 Timothy dealt with order in a functioning church: prayer, elders, deacons, widows. 2 Timothy faces a darker horizon: “But know this, that in the last days, grievous times will come” (3:1). People will love themselves and money more than God, and many will turn from the truth to myths. Paul's answer is not to change the message but to cling to it: Scripture is God-breathed and sufficient, so “preach the word; be urgent in season and out of season” (4:2)."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"Guard the Deposit, Endure the Hardship, Finish the Race","anchorRef":"2 Timothy 1:14; 2:2–3; 4:7","paragraphs":["Three images carry the whole letter. The first is a deposit: “That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” (1:14). The gospel is a treasure entrusted to Timothy, like valuables left with a guard. He did not invent it and he must not alter it. And he cannot guard it in his own strength, but only ‘through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us’.","The second is a soldier, an athlete and a farmer: “You therefore must endure hardship, as a good soldier of Christ Jesus” (2:3). The soldier endures and stays focused on pleasing his commander; the athlete competes by the rules; the hard-working farmer is first to share the crops (2:4–6). Each picture says that faithfulness is costly and that it is rewarded.","The third is a race finished: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (4:7). Paul is about to die. He does not look back on a comfortable life, a large fortune or a famous name. He looks back on faithfulness — to the fight, the course and the faith — and he looks forward to the crown.","Holding the three together is a relay: “The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (2:2). Paul to Timothy, Timothy to faithful men, faithful men to others — four generations in one verse. You are part of that relay. Someone handed the gospel to you; you are called to guard it, endure for it, and pass it on."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 4 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"God Has Given a Spirit of Power, Not Fear","body":"Timothy may have been timid, and the times were dangerous. Paul reminds him: “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (1:7). Fear is not from God. The Holy Spirit gives courage to speak, love to persevere and self-control to stay steady under pressure. Whatever you face, you have not been left with a spirit of cowardice."},{"title":"Scripture Is God-Breathed and Enough","body":"In grievous times, Paul points Timothy back to the Scriptures he had known since childhood: “Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for instruction in righteousness, that the man of God may be complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work” (3:16–17). God's word is not merely inspiring; it is breathed out by God, and it fully equips His people."},{"title":"Faithfulness Will Be Rewarded","body":"Paul faced abandonment, injustice and death, yet he wrote with confidence: “From now on, there is stored up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day; and not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing” (4:8). The crown is not only for apostles; it is for everyone who loves the Lord's appearing. Finishing faithfully matters."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"What Would Your Last Letter Say?","body":"If you knew you had only weeks to live, what would you write to the people you love most? What truths would you most want them to hold on to? Paul's last letter shows what mattered most to him. As you read, ask God to show you what should matter most to you — and to help you live now in a way that you will be glad of at the end."},{"title":"Where Are You Tempted to Be Ashamed?","body":"Paul repeatedly tells Timothy not to be ashamed. Think honestly: where are you tempted to hide your faith, avoid association with suffering believers, or soften the gospel to avoid embarrassment? Bring that to God before you start, and ask Him to fill you with the spirit of power, love and self-control."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Father, I come to the last letter of Your apostle Paul, written from chains as he faced death. Thank You for the gospel that was handed down to me through faithful people across the centuries. Make me faithful too. Stir up the gift You have given me. Drive out the spirit of fear, and fill me with power, love and self-control. Keep me from being ashamed of the testimony of my Lord or of those who suffer for Him. Help me guard the good deposit through the Holy Spirit who lives in me, and pass it on to others who will teach others also. Make me a good soldier, an honest athlete and a hard-working farmer in Your service. Root me in Your God-breathed Scriptures, so that I am complete and equipped for every good work. Help me preach the word in season and out of season. And when my time comes, let me be able to say, ‘I have fought the good fight, I have finished the course, I have kept the faith’, because I have loved Your appearing. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Timothy","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Not a Spirit of Fear — Stir Up the Gift and Guard the Good Deposit","themeTitle":"“For God Didn’t Give Us a Spirit of Fear, but of Power, Love, and Self-Control”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, according to the promise of the life which is in Christ Jesus,","understanding":"Paul, ἀπόστολος, an apostle, διὰ θελήματος θεοῦ, “through the will of God” — not by his own ambition. And κατ᾽ ἐπαγγελίαν ζωῆς, “according to the promise of the life which is in Christ Jesus.” Written by a man about to be executed, the first sentence speaks of life — ζωή from the life family: life itself, God's life. Death row cannot cancel the promise of life.","words":[{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"}]},{"v":2,"text":"to Timothy, my beloved child: Grace, mercy, and peace, from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Lord.","understanding":"To Timothy, ἀγαπητῷ τέκνῳ, “my beloved child” — in 1 Timothy it was ‘my true child’; now it is ‘my beloved child’, tender words from a father near death. The same greeting as before: χάρις, ἔλεος, εἰρήνη, “Grace, mercy, and peace,” from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Lord.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":3,"text":"I thank God, whom I serve as my forefathers did, with a pure conscience. How unceasing is my memory of you in my petitions, night and day","understanding":"χάριν ἔχω τῷ θεῷ, “I thank God,” ᾧ λατρεύω, “whom I serve” — λατρεύω is the word for worship-service, the service a priest offers to God — ἀπὸ προγόνων, “as my forefathers did,” ἐν καθαρᾷ συνειδήσει, “with a pure conscience.” Paul sees continuity between his Jewish heritage and his faith in Christ. Then: ἀδιάλειπτον ἔχω τὴν περὶ σοῦ μνείαν ἐν ταῖς δεήσεσίν μου, “How unceasing is my memory of you in my petitions” — δέησις from the pray family: specific requests — νυκτὸς καὶ ἡμέρας, “night and day.” A prisoner who could do little else could still pray.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"thank"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G4269","hebrew":"πρόγονος","translit":"prógonos","english":"forefathers"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G89","hebrew":"ἀδιαλείπτως","translit":"adialeíptōs","english":"unceasing"},{"strongs":"G3417","hebrew":"μνεία","translit":"mneía","english":"memory"},{"strongs":"G1162","hebrew":"δέησις","translit":"déēsis","english":"petitions"}]},{"v":4,"text":"longing to see you, remembering your tears, that I may be filled with joy;","understanding":"ἐπιποθῶν σε ἰδεῖν, “longing to see you” — ἐπιποθέω, intense yearning. μεμνημένος σου τῶν δακρύων, “remembering your tears” — probably the tears at their last parting. ἵνα χαρᾶς πληρωθῶ, “that I may be filled with joy.” The great apostle was a man with deep human affections, and he was not ashamed of them.","words":[{"strongs":"G1971","hebrew":"ἐπιποθέω","translit":"epipothéō","english":"longing"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G3403","hebrew":"μιμνήσκω","translit":"mimnḗskō","english":"remembering"},{"strongs":"G1145","hebrew":"δακρύω","translit":"dakrýō","english":"tears"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"filled"}]},{"v":5,"text":"having been reminded of the sincere faith that is in you; which lived first in your grandmother Lois, and your mother Eunice, and, I am persuaded, in you also.","understanding":"ὑπόμνησιν λαβὼν τῆς ἐν σοὶ ἀνυποκρίτου πίστεως, “having been reminded of the sincere faith that is in you” — ἀνυπόκριτος, unhypocritical, faith without a mask (1 Timothy 1:5). This faith ἐνῴκησεν πρῶτον, “lived first” — dwelt, made its home — ἐν τῇ μάμμῃ σου Λωΐδι, “in your grandmother Lois,” καὶ τῇ μητρί σου Εὐνίκῃ, “and your mother Eunice,” and, πέπεισμαι, “I am persuaded,” in you also. Faith was passed down three generations through the women of the family.","words":[{"strongs":"G5280","hebrew":"ὑπόμνησις","translit":"hypómnēsis","english":"reminded"},{"strongs":"G505","hebrew":"ἀνυπόκριτος","translit":"anypókritos","english":"sincere"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1774","hebrew":"ἐνοικέω","translit":"enoikéō","english":"lived"},{"strongs":"G3090","hebrew":"Λωΐς","translit":"Lōḯs","english":"Lois"},{"strongs":"G2131","hebrew":"Εὐνίκη","translit":"Euníkē","english":"Eunice"},{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"persuaded"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For this cause, I remind you that you should stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands.","understanding":"δι᾽ ἣν αἰτίαν ἀναμιμνῄσκω σε, “For this cause, I remind you” — because your faith is real — ἀναζωπυρεῖν τὸ χάρισμα τοῦ θεοῦ, “that you should stir up the gift of God.” ἀναζωπυρέω means to rekindle a fire, to fan embers back into flame. The χάρισμα, grace-gift, was given διὰ τῆς ἐπιθέσεως τῶν χειρῶν μου, “through the laying on of my hands” (v. 6; compare 1 Timothy 4:14). Gifts can burn low through fear, discouragement or neglect; they must be fanned again.","words":[{"strongs":"G363","hebrew":"ἀναμιμνήσκω","translit":"anamimnḗskō","english":"remind"},{"strongs":"G329","hebrew":"ἀναζωπυρέω","translit":"anazōpyréō","english":"stir up"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G1936","hebrew":"ἐπίθεσις","translit":"epíthesis","english":"laying on"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control.","understanding":"The reason Timothy can rekindle the fire: οὐ γὰρ ἔδωκεν ἡμῖν ὁ θεὸς πνεῦμα δειλίας, “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear” — δειλία is cowardice, timidity, the fear that shrinks back. ἀλλὰ δυνάμεως, “but of power” — δύναμις from the power family: God's inherent ability to accomplish; καὶ ἀγάπης, “love” — the love family's ἀγάπη; καὶ σωφρονισμοῦ, “and self-control” — a disciplined, sound mind. Power without love becomes harsh; love without self-control becomes soft. The Spirit gives all three.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G1167","hebrew":"δειλία","translit":"deilía","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4995","hebrew":"σωφρονισμός","translit":"sōphronismós","english":"self-control"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Therefore don’t be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner; but endure hardship for the Good News according to the power of God,","understanding":"μὴ οὖν ἐπαισχυνθῇς, “Therefore don’t be ashamed” of τὸ μαρτύριον τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν, “the testimony of our Lord” — the gospel about Him — μηδὲ ἐμὲ τὸν δέσμιον αὐτοῦ, “nor of me his prisoner.” Paul is not Caesar's prisoner but Christ's. Instead: συγκακοπάθησον τῷ εὐαγγελίῳ, “endure hardship for the Good News” — literally ‘suffer evil together with’ the gospel — κατὰ δύναμιν θεοῦ, “according to the power of God.” The same δύναμις of verse 7 enables suffering.","words":[{"strongs":"G1870","hebrew":"ἐπαισχύνομαι","translit":"epaischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G1198","hebrew":"δέσμιος","translit":"désmios","english":"prisoner"},{"strongs":"G4777","hebrew":"συγκακοπαθέω","translit":"synkakopathéō","english":"endure hardship"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"}]},{"v":9,"text":"who saved us and called us with a holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given to us in Christ Jesus before times eternal,","understanding":"The God who empowers is the God “who saved (σώσαντος) us and called (καλέσαντος) us with a holy (ἁγίᾳ) calling (κλήσει).” Not κατὰ τὰ ἔργα ἡμῶν, “not according to our works,” ἀλλὰ κατὰ ἰδίαν πρόθεσιν καὶ χάριν, “but according to his own purpose and grace,” which was given to us in Christ Jesus πρὸ χρόνων αἰωνίων, “before times eternal” — a time-family phrase: before the ages of time began. Salvation was planned and given in Christ before creation.","words":[{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G2821","hebrew":"κλῆσις","translit":"klēsis","english":"calling"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G4286","hebrew":"πρόθεσις","translit":"próthesis","english":"purpose"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"times"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":10,"text":"but has now been revealed by the appearing of our Savior, Christ Jesus, who abolished death, and brought life and immortality to light through the Good News.","understanding":"What was given before time φανερωθεῖσαν δὲ νῦν, “has now been revealed,” διὰ τῆς ἐπιφανείας τοῦ σωτῆρος ἡμῶν Χριστοῦ Ἰησοῦ, “by the appearing of our Savior, Christ Jesus” — ἐπιφάνεια, the shining forth; here His first coming. Two achievements: καταργήσαντος μὲν τὸν θάνατον, “who abolished death” — made it powerless, put it out of action — φωτίσαντος δὲ ζωὴν καὶ ἀφθαρσίαν, “and brought life and immortality to light” — ἀφθαρσία, incorruptibility. A condemned man writes that death has been abolished.","words":[{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G2015","hebrew":"ἐπιφάνεια","translit":"epipháneia","english":"appearing"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G2673","hebrew":"καταργέω","translit":"katargéō","english":"abolished"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"brought to light"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G861","hebrew":"ἀφθαρσία","translit":"aphtharsía","english":"immortality"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For this, I was appointed as a preacher, an apostle, and a teacher of the Gentiles.","understanding":"εἰς ὃ ἐτέθην ἐγὼ κῆρυξ καὶ ἀπόστολος καὶ διδάσκαλος, “For this, I was appointed as a preacher (a herald), an apostle, and a teacher of the Gentiles” (the WEB follows manuscripts that add ‘of the Gentiles’). The same three roles as 1 Timothy 2:7: herald announcing the King's news, apostle sent with authority, teacher explaining it.","words":[{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"appointed"},{"strongs":"G2783","hebrew":"κῆρυξ","translit":"kēryx","english":"preacher"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teacher"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For this cause I also suffer these things. Yet I am not ashamed, for I know him whom I have believed, and I am persuaded that he is able to guard that which I have committed to him against that day.","understanding":"δι᾽ ἣν αἰτίαν καὶ ταῦτα πάσχω, “For this cause I also suffer these things” — prison and chains — ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἐπαισχύνομαι, “Yet I am not ashamed.” Why? οἶδα γὰρ ᾧ πεπίστευκα, “for I know (οἶδα, from the know family: settled, certain knowledge) him whom I have believed” — not ‘what’ but ‘whom’: faith rests on a Person. καὶ πέπεισμαι, “and I am persuaded,” that He is able φυλάξαι τὴν παραθήκην μου, “to guard that which I have committed to him” — παραθήκη, the deposit — εἰς ἐκείνην τὴν ἡμέραν, “against that day.” Paul has entrusted his life to Christ, and Christ will keep it safe until the day of His appearing.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffer"},{"strongs":"G1870","hebrew":"ἐπαισχύνομαι","translit":"epaischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believed"},{"strongs":"G3982","hebrew":"πείθω","translit":"peíthō","english":"persuaded"},{"strongs":"G1415","hebrew":"δυνατός","translit":"dynatós","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"guard"},{"strongs":"G3866","hebrew":"παραθήκη","translit":"parathḗkē","english":"committed"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Hold the pattern of sound words which you have heard from me, in faith and love which is in Christ Jesus.","understanding":"ὑποτύπωσιν ἔχε ὑγιαινόντων λόγων, “Hold the pattern of sound words” — ὑποτύπωσις, a sketch, an outline (the ‘example’ of 1 Timothy 1:16); ὑγιαίνω, healthy. The apostolic teaching is a pattern to follow, not a rough idea to be improved. And hold it ἐν πίστει καὶ ἀγάπῃ, “in faith and love which is in Christ Jesus” — truth held without love becomes harsh; truth held without faith becomes dead.","words":[{"strongs":"G5296","hebrew":"ὑποτύπωσις","translit":"hypotýpōsis","english":"pattern"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"}]},{"v":14,"text":"That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us.","understanding":"τὴν καλὴν παραθήκην φύλαξον, “That good thing which was committed to you, guard” — the beautiful deposit (καλός, from the good family). Christ guards what Paul entrusted to Him (v. 12); Timothy must guard what Christ has entrusted to him. And not alone: διὰ πνεύματος ἁγίου τοῦ ἐνοικοῦντος ἐν ἡμῖν, “through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” — ἐνοικέω, to live at home inside. The Spirit who lives in believers is the true guardian of the truth.","words":[{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3866","hebrew":"παραθήκη","translit":"parathḗkē","english":"committed"},{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"guard"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1774","hebrew":"ἐνοικέω","translit":"enoikéō","english":"dwells"}]},{"v":15,"text":"This you know, that all who are in Asia turned away from me; of whom are Phygelus and Hermogenes.","understanding":"A painful reality: “This you know, that all who are in Asia ἀπεστράφησάν με, turned away from me” — perhaps when Paul was arrested, the believers of the province of Asia, where he had worked so long, distanced themselves from him. Two names: Φύγελος, Phygelus, and Ἑρμογένης, Hermogenes. We know nothing else about them — their only memorial is their desertion.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G654","hebrew":"ἀποστρέφω","translit":"apostréphō","english":"turned away"},{"strongs":"G5436","hebrew":"Φύγελλος","translit":"Phýgellos","english":"Phygelus"},{"strongs":"G2061","hebrew":"Ἑρμογένης","translit":"Hermogénēs","english":"Hermogenes"}]},{"v":16,"text":"May the Lord grant mercy to the house of Onesiphorus, for he often refreshed me, and was not ashamed of my chain,","understanding":"In contrast, a faithful friend: “May the Lord grant mercy (ἔλεος) to the house of Ὀνησιφόρου, Onesiphorus” — his name means ‘bringing profit’ — “for he often ἀνέψυξεν, refreshed me” — literally ‘cooled me off’, gave relief like a cool breeze — “and was not ashamed (ἐπαισχύνθη) of my chain (ἅλυσιν).” Being associated with a condemned prisoner was dangerous; Onesiphorus did not care.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G3683","hebrew":"Ὀνησίφορος","translit":"Onēsíphoros","english":"Onesiphorus"},{"strongs":"G404","hebrew":"ἀναψύχω","translit":"anapsýchō","english":"refreshed"},{"strongs":"G1870","hebrew":"ἐπαισχύνομαι","translit":"epaischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G254","hebrew":"ἅλυσις","translit":"hálysis","english":"chain"}]},{"v":17,"text":"but when he was in Rome, he sought me diligently, and found me","understanding":"ἀλλὰ γενόμενος ἐν Ῥώμῃ σπουδαιότερον ἐζήτησέν με καὶ εὗρεν, “but when he was in Rome, he sought me diligently, and found me.” Finding a particular prisoner in Nero's Rome was not easy, and asking around for a Christian prisoner was risky. Onesiphorus kept searching until he found him. True friendship searches for those in chains.","words":[{"strongs":"G4706","hebrew":"σπουδαιότερον","translit":"spoudaióteron","english":"diligently"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"sought"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"}]},{"v":18,"text":"(the Lord grant to him to find the Lord’s mercy in that day); and in how many things he served at Ephesus, you know very well.","understanding":"A prayer in parentheses: δῴη αὐτῷ ὁ κύριος εὑρεῖν ἔλεος παρὰ κυρίου ἐν ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ, “(the Lord grant to him to find the Lord’s mercy in that day).” He found Paul; may he find mercy. Because Paul speaks of his household rather than of him (v. 16; 4:19), some think Onesiphorus had died; the text does not say so, and no teaching should be built on this one prayer. Paul adds that Timothy knows βέλτιον, “very well,” how much he διηκόνησεν, “served,” at Ephesus — the verb of the servant family's διάκονος.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"find"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"served"},{"strongs":"G2181","hebrew":"Ἔφεσος","translit":"Éphesos","english":"Ephesus"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Fanning the Embers","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture a fire in a stone hearth on a cold night. Earlier it blazed and warmed the whole room, but it has been left alone. Now there are only a few glowing embers under a layer of grey ash. The room is getting cold. Someone kneels down, brushes away the ash, adds some dry kindling and blows gently. A flicker, then a flame, then the whole fire springs back to life. The fuel was there all along; it only needed stirring.","meaning":"Paul writes, “I remind you that you should stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands. For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (vv. 6–7). The word ἀναζωπυρέω means to rekindle a fire. Fear, discouragement and neglect cover our gifts with ash. The Spirit has not left; the gift has not been withdrawn. What has God given you that has burned low? Brush off the ash of fear and fan it back into flame."},{"title":"The Banker and the Deposit","verseRef":"v. 12-14","image":"Picture a traveller in the ancient world about to go on a dangerous journey. He takes everything valuable he owns — gold, documents, the deeds to his house — to a trusted guardian and leaves it there for safekeeping. As he sets out, he does not worry about his treasure; he knows who has it. Now picture Paul in prison. He has deposited his life, his soul, his future with Christ, and he is not anxious. And Christ, in turn, has placed a deposit in Timothy's hands — the gospel — and tells him to guard it.","meaning":"Two deposits are pictured here. First: “for I know him whom I have believed, and I am persuaded that he is able to guard that which I have committed to him against that day” (v. 12). Second: “That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” (v. 14). Christ keeps you; you keep His truth. You can rest in His keeping and work at your guarding, knowing the Holy Spirit lives in you to help."},{"title":"The Friend Who Kept Searching","verseRef":"v. 15-18","image":"Picture the busy streets of Rome. Onesiphorus arrives from Ephesus and begins asking where the Christian prisoner Paul is held. People look away; some warn him that it is dangerous to ask. He goes from prison to prison, guardhouse to guardhouse, day after day. Finally, in a dark cell, he finds the old apostle in chains. He sits down beside him, brings food and news, and for the first time in weeks Paul feels refreshed, like someone who has been given cool water in the heat.","meaning":"In contrast to those in Asia who turned away (v. 15), Paul remembers Onesiphorus: “for he often refreshed me, and was not ashamed of my chain, but when he was in Rome, he sought me diligently, and found me” (vv. 16–17). Faithfulness is often shown in simple, costly loyalty: visiting the imprisoned, standing with the ashamed, searching for the forgotten. Who in your life is ‘in chains’ — sick, imprisoned, rejected, lonely? Be their Onesiphorus."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting and Thanksgiving (v. 1-5)","body":"Paul, an apostle by God's will according to the promise of life in Christ, greets his beloved son Timothy, thanks God as he remembers him constantly in prayer, longs to see him, recalls his tears, and remembers his sincere faith, first in Lois and Eunice."},{"label":"Rekindle the Gift (v. 6-7)","body":"Timothy must fan into flame the gift of God received through Paul's hands, because God gave not a spirit of fear but of power, love and self-control."},{"label":"Do Not Be Ashamed — Share in Suffering (v. 8-12)","body":"Do not be ashamed of the testimony about the Lord or of Paul His prisoner, but share in suffering for the gospel by God's power — the God who saved and called us by His own purpose and grace, given in Christ before time began and now revealed through Christ's appearing, who abolished death and brought life and immortality to light; for this Paul suffers, unashamed, knowing whom he has believed."},{"label":"Guard the Good Deposit (v. 13-14)","body":"Timothy must follow the pattern of sound words in faith and love, and guard the good deposit through the indwelling Holy Spirit."},{"label":"Deserters and a Faithful Friend (v. 15-18)","body":"All in Asia turned away from Paul, including Phygelus and Hermogenes; but Onesiphorus often refreshed him, was not ashamed of his chains, searched for him in Rome and found him; may the Lord grant him mercy on that day."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Father's Last Letter","paragraphs":["2 Timothy begins with life: “Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, according to the promise of the life which is in Christ Jesus” (v. 1). It is striking that a man facing execution opens with the promise of life. Then comes warmth: Timothy is ‘my beloved child’ (v. 2). Paul thanks God for him and prays for him “night and day” (v. 3). He remembers Timothy's tears at their last parting and longs to see him again, “that I may be filled with joy” (v. 4). The great theologian was also a loving spiritual father.","Paul remembers Timothy's faith and where it came from: “the sincere faith that is in you; which lived first in your grandmother Lois, and your mother Eunice, and, I am persuaded, in you also” (v. 5). Timothy's father was a Greek and apparently not a believer (Acts 16:1), but faithful women passed on the faith to him from childhood (3:15). Never underestimate the influence of a godly grandmother or mother. The faith that would one day lead the church in Ephesus was first taught at a kitchen table in Lystra.","Because Timothy's faith is real, Paul can urge him: “I remind you that you should stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands” (v. 6). The gift had not disappeared, but it seems to have burned low. And the reason is given in the next verse: fear. “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (v. 7). Timothy faced a dangerous time, opposition in the church and the imminent loss of his mentor. Paul reminds him that the Spirit he received is not a spirit of timidity but of power to act, love to keep serving, and a disciplined mind to stay steady."]},{"heading":"Not Ashamed","paragraphs":["The key word of chapter 1 is ‘ashamed’. Paul writes: “Therefore don’t be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner; but endure hardship for the Good News according to the power of God” (v. 8). In Nero's Rome, being linked to a condemned Christian prisoner was socially and physically dangerous. The temptation was to keep quiet and keep one's distance. Paul calls Timothy instead to share in suffering, and he grounds it in the power of God.","Then he reminds Timothy what this gospel is: God “who saved us and called us with a holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given to us in Christ Jesus before times eternal, but has now been revealed by the appearing of our Savior, Christ Jesus, who abolished death, and brought life and immortality to light through the Good News” (vv. 9–10). Salvation is by grace, planned before time, revealed in Christ. And Christ has abolished death — made it powerless. For a man facing execution, that is not theory; it is his hope.","Paul applies it to himself: “For this cause I also suffer these things. Yet I am not ashamed, for I know him whom I have believed, and I am persuaded that he is able to guard that which I have committed to him against that day” (v. 12). Paul does not say ‘I know what I have believed’, though he did; he says ‘whom’. Faith rests on a Person. And because Paul has entrusted himself to Christ, he is free from fear. The One who holds him is able to keep him."]},{"heading":"Guard the Deposit, Remember the Faithful","paragraphs":["Paul gives Timothy two commands about the truth. First: “Hold the pattern of sound words which you have heard from me, in faith and love which is in Christ Jesus” (v. 13). The apostolic teaching is a pattern, a model to follow closely, and it must be held in faith and love, not with cold harshness. Second: “That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” (v. 14). The deposit is the gospel; the guard is the Spirit living within believers. Timothy does not guard it alone.","Then come two contrasting examples. “This you know, that all who are in Asia turned away from me; of whom are Phygelus and Hermogenes” (v. 15). When Paul was arrested, many who once followed him distanced themselves. It must have been one of the deepest pains of his life. But there was a faithful friend: “May the Lord grant mercy to the house of Onesiphorus, for he often refreshed me, and was not ashamed of my chain, but when he was in Rome, he sought me diligently, and found me” (vv. 16–17).","Onesiphorus is the living example of verse 8. He was not ashamed of Paul's chains. He searched until he found him and brought him refreshment. Paul prays for him: “the Lord grant to him to find the Lord’s mercy in that day” (v. 18). Onesiphorus looked for Paul and found him; Paul prays that he will look for mercy on judgment day and find it. Faithfulness to Christ's suffering servants is noticed by God and will not be forgotten."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Courage, Calling and Christ's Victory Over Death","paragraphs":["Paul's refusal to be ashamed echoes his great declaration in Romans: “For I am not ashamed of the Good News of Christ, because it is the power of God for salvation for everyone who believes” (Romans 1:16). Jesus Himself warned, “For whoever will be ashamed of me and of my words in this adulterous and sinful generation, the Son of Man also will be ashamed of him, when he comes in his Father’s glory, with the holy angels” (Mark 8:38).","The truth that salvation is by grace and not works runs throughout Paul's letters: “for by grace you have been saved through faith, and that not of yourselves; it is the gift of God, not of works, that no one would boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). And God's plan before time appears in Ephesians: “even as he chose us in him before the foundation of the world” (Ephesians 1:4).","Christ's abolition of death is the climax of 1 Corinthians 15: “The last enemy that will be abolished is death” (1 Corinthians 15:26), and “Death, where is your sting? Hades, where is your victory?” (1 Corinthians 15:55). Hebrews explains how: Christ shared our humanity “that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil, and might deliver all of them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage” (Hebrews 2:14–15). That is why Paul could face execution without fear."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Stir up / rekindle","hebrew":"ἀναζωπυρέω","translit":"anazōpyréō","strongs":"G329","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “to re-enkindle.” It appears only here in the New Testament. It combines ἀνά, again, ζωός, alive, and πῦρ, fire: to make the fire alive again. The gift God gave Timothy was not lost, but it had cooled. Gifts are not maintained automatically; they need to be stirred, used and fanned into flame. Fear smothers gifts; faith in the God who gives power, love and self-control rekindles them."},{"english":"Fear / timidity / cowardice","hebrew":"δειλία","translit":"deilía","strongs":"G1167","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “timidity.” It appears only here in the New Testament. Its related adjective δειλός describes the ‘fearful’ disciples in the storm (Matthew 8:26) and the ‘cowardly’ who are excluded from the new creation (Revelation 21:8). It is not healthy caution but the fear that makes a person shrink back from obedience. God has not given His people this spirit. Fear may come, but it is not from Him."},{"english":"Self-control / sound mind","hebrew":"σωφρονισμός","translit":"sōphronismós","strongs":"G4995","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “discipline, i.e. self-control.” It appears only here in the New Testament. It belongs to the σώφρων family — sound-minded, sensible, self-controlled — words that run through the pastoral letters (1 Timothy 3:2; Titus 2:2–6). The Spirit gives not only power and love but a disciplined, clear mind that is not swept away by panic or emotion. Courage without self-control becomes rashness; the Spirit gives both."},{"english":"Be ashamed","hebrew":"ἐπαισχύνομαι","translit":"epaischýnomai","strongs":"G1870","verses":"v. 8, 12, 16","insight":"Strong's: “to feel shame for something.” It appears three times in this chapter: Timothy must not be ashamed (v. 8); Paul is not ashamed (v. 12); Onesiphorus was not ashamed (v. 16). In Romans 1:16 Paul says he is not ashamed of the gospel, and Jesus warned of being ashamed of Him (Mark 8:38). Shame is a powerful weapon against witness. The cure is knowing whom you have believed and remembering His power."},{"english":"Deposit / thing committed in trust","hebrew":"παραθήκη","translit":"parathḗkē","strongs":"G3866","verses":"v. 12, 14","insight":"Strong's: “a deposit, i.e. (figuratively) trust.” In verse 12 it is what Paul has entrusted to Christ — his life and future — which Christ will guard. In verse 14 it is what Christ has entrusted to Timothy — the gospel — which Timothy must guard by the Spirit. The same word appears in 1 Timothy 6:20. God keeps us; we keep His truth. Both are held in trust until ‘that day’."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: the gift that has cooled in you, the fear that holds you back, and the friends who need you.","items":[{"title":"Fan the Flame","body":"Is there a gift God has given you — teaching, encouraging, serving, giving, leading, praying — that has burned low? Perhaps fear, discouragement, criticism or busyness has covered it with ash. The Spirit is not condemning you; He is inviting you to stir it up. Ask Him to show you one practical step to use that gift again this week, and take it."},{"title":"Not a Spirit of Fear","body":"Fear may be shaping your decisions: fear of people, of failure, of suffering, of being associated with unpopular truth. The Spirit reminds you that God did not give you a spirit of fear, but of power, love and self-control. Name the fear honestly before Him. Then ask Him to replace it with His power to act, His love to serve, and His sound mind to stay steady."},{"title":"Be an Onesiphorus","body":"While many deserted Paul, Onesiphorus searched for him and refreshed him, unashamed of his chains. The Spirit may be bringing someone to mind who is isolated, imprisoned, sick, rejected or forgotten. Go and find them. Bring refreshment. Your faithfulness to them is faithfulness to Christ."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Gift Check","body":"List the spiritual gifts and abilities God has given you. Mark any that you have stopped using. Ask why — fear, discouragement, neglect? Choose one to rekindle this month (v. 6)."},{"title":"The Shame Check","body":"In what situations are you tempted to hide your faith or distance yourself from other believers who are suffering or unpopular (v. 8)? Write down one and ask God for courage."},{"title":"The Heritage Check","body":"Who passed the faith on to you (v. 5)? Thank God for them. Then ask: to whom am I passing it on?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write to Your Timothy","body":"Paul wrote this letter to his spiritual son. Think of someone younger in faith whom you care about. Write them a letter or message this week: tell them you thank God for them, remind them of the sincere faith you see in them, encourage them not to be afraid, and urge them to use their gifts. Pray for them daily for a month."},{"title":"A Visit to the Chained","body":"Follow Onesiphorus. This month, visit or contact someone who is ‘in chains’: in prison, in hospital, housebound, or isolated because of their faith. Take something practical — food, a book, help with a task — and spend time listening. Pray with them if they are open. Afterwards, ask God how to make this a regular part of your life."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Father, thank You for the promise of life in Christ Jesus, which no prison or death can cancel. Thank You for those who passed the faith on to me. Make my faith sincere, without a mask. Fan into flame the gift You have given me, and remove the ash of fear and discouragement. Thank You that You have not given me a spirit of fear, but of power, love and self-control. Keep me from being ashamed of the testimony of my Lord or of those who suffer for Him. Give me strength to share in suffering for the gospel by Your power. Thank You that You saved me and called me with a holy calling, not because of my works but because of Your own purpose and grace, given in Christ before time began. Thank You that Jesus abolished death and brought life and immortality to light. I know whom I have believed, and I am persuaded that He is able to guard what I have entrusted to Him until that day. Help me hold to the pattern of sound words in faith and love, and guard the good deposit through the Holy Spirit who lives in me. Make me faithful like Onesiphorus, refreshing those in chains. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Timothy Joins Paul at Lystra","ref":"Acts 16:1-5","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 5-6","summary":"On his second missionary journey, Paul came to Derbe and Lystra, where he met a disciple named Timothy, “the son of a Jewess who believed; but his father was a Greek” (Acts 16:1). The believers in Lystra and Iconium spoke well of him, and Paul wanted Timothy to travel with him. Timothy's faith had been nurtured by his mother Eunice and grandmother Lois, and now it would carry him into a lifetime of ministry beside Paul. 2 Timothy 1 looks back on that beginning: the sincere faith first found in his grandmother and mother, and the gift given when Paul laid hands on him."},{"title":"Peter's Denial and Restoration","ref":"Luke 22:54-62","relatedVerses":"v. 8, 12, 15","summary":"On the night Jesus was arrested, Peter followed at a distance into the high priest's courtyard. Three times people recognised him as one of Jesus' followers, and three times he denied knowing Him. As the rooster crowed, the Lord turned and looked at Peter, and Peter went out and wept bitterly. Fear and shame made a courageous disciple deny his Lord. Yet Jesus restored him, and Peter later preached boldly and suffered for Christ. 2 Timothy 1 calls believers not to be ashamed of the testimony of the Lord or of His suffering servants — and it shows that even those who have been ashamed can be restored and strengthened by the Spirit of power, love and self-control."},{"title":"Ebed-melech Rescues Jeremiah","ref":"Jeremiah 38:1-13","relatedVerses":"v. 16-18","summary":"When the prophet Jeremiah warned that Jerusalem would fall, the officials threw him into a muddy cistern to die, and he sank into the mud. No one in the royal court spoke up — except Ebed-melech, an Ethiopian servant in the king's house. He went to the king and pleaded for Jeremiah, then took thirty men, old rags and ropes, and lifted the prophet out of the pit. Later God promised Ebed-melech that he would be delivered when the city fell, because he trusted in the LORD. Like Onesiphorus, he was not ashamed of a persecuted servant of God, and he took risks to bring relief. God remembered him."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Timothy","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Soldier, Athlete, Farmer, Workman — Pass It On and Rightly Handle the Word","themeTitle":"“The Things Which You Have Heard From Me … Commit the Same to Faithful Men”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"You therefore, my child, be strengthened in the grace that is in Christ Jesus.","understanding":"σὺ οὖν, τέκνον μου, “You therefore, my child” — in contrast to the deserters of 1:15 — ἐνδυναμοῦ, “be strengthened” — ἐνδυναμόω from the power family: be continually empowered from within — ἐν τῇ χάριτι, “in the grace that is in Christ Jesus.” Strength for ministry does not come from willpower but from grace. The command is passive: let yourself be strengthened.","words":[{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G1743","hebrew":"ἐνδυναμόω","translit":"endynamóō","english":"be strengthened"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]},{"v":2,"text":"The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also.","understanding":"The great relay verse: “The things which you have heard (ἤκουσας) from me among many witnesses (μαρτύρων), commit (παράθου — deposit, entrust) the same to faithful (πιστοῖς) men, who will be able (ἱκανοί — competent, qualified) to teach (διδάξαι) others also.” Four generations: Paul, Timothy, faithful people, others. The gospel spreads through people who receive it, live it and pass it on. Faithfulness comes before ability.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witnesses"},{"strongs":"G3908","hebrew":"παρατίθημι","translit":"paratíthēmi","english":"commit"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G2425","hebrew":"ἱκανός","translit":"hikanós","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teach"}]},{"v":3,"text":"You therefore must endure hardship, as a good soldier of Christ Jesus.","understanding":"The first picture: the soldier. συγκακοπάθησον, “endure hardship” — share in suffering (the WEB follows manuscripts reading simply ‘endure hardship’) — ὡς καλὸς στρατιώτης Χριστοῦ Ἰησοῦ, “as a good soldier of Christ Jesus.” καλός from the good family: a fine, excellent soldier. Soldiers expect hardship; it comes with the uniform.","words":[{"strongs":"G2553","hebrew":"κακοπαθέω","translit":"kakopathéō","english":"endure hardship"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G4757","hebrew":"στρατιώτης","translit":"stratiṓtēs","english":"soldier"}]},{"v":4,"text":"No soldier on duty entangles himself in the affairs of life, that he may please him who enrolled him as a soldier.","understanding":"“No soldier on duty (στρατευόμενος) entangles himself (ἐμπλέκεται — gets wrapped up, tangled) in the affairs (πραγματείαις) of life (βίου).” βίος from the life family: the business of everyday earthly life — trade, possessions, civilian concerns. A soldier on campaign is not forbidden to eat or sleep, but he does not get tangled in business that distracts from his mission, ἵνα τῷ στρατολογήσαντι ἀρέσῃ, “that he may please him who enrolled him as a soldier.” The question is not ‘Is this allowed?’ but ‘Does this entangle me?’","words":[{"strongs":"G4754","hebrew":"στρατεύομαι","translit":"strateúomai","english":"on duty"},{"strongs":"G1707","hebrew":"ἐμπλέκω","translit":"emplékō","english":"entangles"},{"strongs":"G4230","hebrew":"πραγματεία","translit":"pragmateía","english":"affairs"},{"strongs":"G979","hebrew":"βίος","translit":"bíos","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G4758","hebrew":"στρατολογέω","translit":"stratologéō","english":"enrolled him as a soldier"},{"strongs":"G700","hebrew":"ἀρέσκω","translit":"aréskō","english":"please"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Also, if anyone competes in athletics, he isn’t crowned unless he has competed by the rules.","understanding":"The second picture: the athlete. ἐὰν δὲ καὶ ἀθλῇ τις, “Also, if anyone competes in athletics,” οὐ στεφανοῦται, “he isn’t crowned” — the victor's wreath — ἐὰν μὴ νομίμως ἀθλήσῃ, “unless he has competed by the rules.” At the Olympic games, athletes swore to train for ten months and compete fairly; cheaters were disqualified. Christian ministry has rules too: faithfulness, truth, integrity. Shortcuts forfeit the crown.","words":[{"strongs":"G118","hebrew":"ἀθλέω","translit":"athléō","english":"competes"},{"strongs":"G4737","hebrew":"στεφανόω","translit":"stephanóō","english":"crowned"},{"strongs":"G3545","hebrew":"νομίμως","translit":"nomímōs","english":"by the rules"}]},{"v":6,"text":"The farmer who labors must be the first to get a share of the crops.","understanding":"The third picture: the farmer. τὸν κοπιῶντα γεωργόν, “The farmer who labors” — κοπιάω from the work family: toil to exhaustion — δεῖ πρῶτον τῶν καρπῶν μεταλαμβάνειν, “must be the first to get a share of the crops.” The hard-working farmer rightly enjoys the harvest first. Patient, wearying labour in God's field will be rewarded.","words":[{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"labors"},{"strongs":"G1092","hebrew":"γεωργός","translit":"geōrgós","english":"farmer"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"crops"},{"strongs":"G3335","hebrew":"μεταλαμβάνω","translit":"metalambánō","english":"get a share"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Consider what I say, and may the Lord give you understanding in all things.","understanding":"νόει ὃ λέγω, “Consider what I say” — think it through, reflect on the pictures. δώσει γάρ σοι ὁ κύριος σύνεσιν ἐν πᾶσιν, “and may the Lord give you understanding in all things” — σύνεσις, insight that puts things together. Thinking and prayer belong together: we reflect, and the Lord gives understanding.","words":[{"strongs":"G3539","hebrew":"νοιέω","translit":"noiéō","english":"Consider"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G4907","hebrew":"σύνεσις","translit":"sýnesis","english":"understanding"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, of the offspring of David, according to my Good News,","understanding":"μνημόνευε Ἰησοῦν Χριστὸν ἐγηγερμένον ἐκ νεκρῶν, “Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead” — ἐγείρω from the rise family, perfect tense: raised and still alive. ἐκ σπέρματος Δαυίδ, “of the offspring of David” — the promised Messiah-King. This, Paul says, is κατὰ τὸ εὐαγγέλιόν μου, “according to my Good News.” In suffering, the most important thing to remember is not a principle but a Person: the risen King.","words":[{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"Remember"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"risen"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G4690","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","english":"offspring"},{"strongs":"G1138","hebrew":"Δαβίδ","translit":"Dabíd","english":"David"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":9,"text":"in which I suffer hardship to the point of chains as a criminal. But God’s word isn’t chained.","understanding":"For this gospel Paul κακοπαθῶ, “suffers hardship,” μέχρι δεσμῶν ὡς κακοῦργος, “to the point of chains as a criminal” — κακοῦργος, ‘evil-worker’, the word used of the criminals crucified with Jesus (Luke 23:32). “But God’s word (λόγος) isn’t chained (δέδεται).” The messenger can be bound; the message cannot. Paul's letters from prison prove it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2553","hebrew":"κακοπαθέω","translit":"kakopathéō","english":"suffer hardship"},{"strongs":"G1199","hebrew":"δεσμόν","translit":"desmón","english":"chains"},{"strongs":"G2557","hebrew":"κακοῦργος","translit":"kakoûrgos","english":"criminal"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G1210","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","english":"chained"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Therefore I endure all things for the chosen ones’ sake, that they also may obtain the salvation which is in Christ Jesus with eternal glory.","understanding":"“Therefore I endure (ὑπομένω — remain under, bear up) all things for the chosen ones’ sake (ἐκλεκτούς),” so that they also τύχωσιν, “may obtain,” τῆς σωτηρίας, “the salvation which is in Christ Jesus with eternal glory (δόξης αἰωνίου).” Paul's suffering serves God's people: through it the gospel reaches those God has chosen. Election does not make mission unnecessary; it makes mission certain of fruit.","words":[{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endure"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen ones"},{"strongs":"G5177","hebrew":"τυγχάνω","translit":"tynchánō","english":"obtain"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"}]},{"v":11,"text":"This saying is trustworthy: “For if we died with him, we will also live with him.","understanding":"The fourth ‘faithful saying’ (πιστὸς ὁ λόγος), probably an early hymn in four lines. Line one: “For if we died with him (συναπεθάνομεν), we will also live with him (συζήσομεν).” The συν- prefix, ‘with’, ties believers to Christ: united with Him in His death (Romans 6:8), we share His life.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"trustworthy"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"saying"},{"strongs":"G4880","hebrew":"συναποθνήσκω","translit":"synapothnḗskō","english":"died with"},{"strongs":"G4800","hebrew":"συζάω","translit":"syzáō","english":"live with"}]},{"v":12,"text":"If we endure, we will also reign with him. If we deny him, he also will deny us.","understanding":"Line two: “If we endure (ὑπομένομεν), we will also reign with him (συμβασιλεύσομεν).” Endurance now leads to sharing Christ's reign. Line three, a solemn warning, using ἀρνέομαι, to deny or disown: “If we deny him, he also will deny us” (v. 12) — echoing Jesus' words in Matthew 10:33. Persistent, final denial of Christ has eternal consequences.","words":[{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"endure"},{"strongs":"G4821","hebrew":"συμβασιλεύω","translit":"symbasileúō","english":"reign with"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"deny"}]},{"v":13,"text":"If we are faithless, he remains faithful. For he can’t deny himself.”","understanding":"Line four breaks the pattern: εἰ ἀπιστοῦμεν, “If we are faithless” — wavering, failing in faith — ἐκεῖνος πιστὸς μένει, “he remains faithful.” Not ‘he will be faithless to us’ but ‘He stays faithful’. ἀρνήσασθαι γὰρ ἑαυτὸν οὐ δύναται, “For he can’t deny himself.” There is a difference between deliberate denial (v. 12) and weak faithlessness (v. 13). Peter wavered and was restored. Christ's faithfulness rests on His own character, not ours.","words":[{"strongs":"G569","hebrew":"ἀπιστέω","translit":"apistéō","english":"faithless"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"deny"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Remind them of these things, charging them in the sight of the Lord, that they don’t argue about words, to no profit, to the subverting of those who hear.","understanding":"ταῦτα ὑπομίμνῃσκε, “Remind them of these things” — keep reminding — διαμαρτυρόμενος ἐνώπιον τοῦ κυρίου, “charging them in the sight of the Lord,” μὴ λογομαχεῖν, “that they don’t argue about words” — word-battles, the false teachers' habit (1 Timothy 6:4) — ἐπ᾽ οὐδὲν χρήσιμον, “to no profit,” ἐπὶ καταστροφῇ τῶν ἀκουόντων, “to the subverting of those who hear” — καταστροφή, overturning, the root of ‘catastrophe’. Endless verbal battles do not build people up; they knock them down.","words":[{"strongs":"G5279","hebrew":"ὑπομιμνήσκω","translit":"hypomimnḗskō","english":"Remind"},{"strongs":"G1263","hebrew":"διαμαρτύρομαι","translit":"diamartýromai","english":"charging"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in the sight"},{"strongs":"G3054","hebrew":"λογομαχέω","translit":"logomachéō","english":"argue about words"},{"strongs":"G5539","hebrew":"χρήσιμος","translit":"chrḗsimos","english":"profit"},{"strongs":"G2692","hebrew":"καταστροφή","translit":"katastrophḗ","english":"subverting"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Give diligence to present yourself approved by God, a workman who doesn’t need to be ashamed, properly handling the Word of Truth.","understanding":"σπούδασον σεαυτὸν δόκιμον παραστῆσαι τῷ θεῷ, “Give diligence to present yourself approved by God” — δόκιμος, tested and approved, like a coin that has passed the assay. ἐργάτην ἀνεπαίσχυντον, “a workman who doesn’t need to be ashamed” — ἐργάτης from the work family: a labourer whose work stands inspection. ὀρθοτομοῦντα τὸν λόγον τῆς ἀληθείας, “properly handling the Word of Truth” — ὀρθοτομέω means to cut straight, like a road-builder cutting a straight path or a craftsman making a straight cut. Handle Scripture accurately, without twisting it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"Give diligence"},{"strongs":"G1384","hebrew":"δόκιμος","translit":"dókimos","english":"approved"},{"strongs":"G3936","hebrew":"παρίστημι","translit":"parístēmi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G2040","hebrew":"ἐργάτης","translit":"ergátēs","english":"workman"},{"strongs":"G422","hebrew":"ἀνεπαίσχυντος","translit":"anepaíschyntos","english":"doesn’t need to be ashamed"},{"strongs":"G3718","hebrew":"ὀρθοτομέω","translit":"orthotoméō","english":"properly handling"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"Word"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"Truth"}]},{"v":16,"text":"But shun empty chatter, for it will go further in ungodliness,","understanding":"τὰς δὲ βεβήλους κενοφωνίας περιΐστασο, “But shun empty chatter” — κενοφωνία, empty sounds (1 Timothy 6:20); περιΐστημι, to step around, avoid. ἐπὶ πλεῖον γὰρ προκόψουσιν ἀσεβείας, “for it will go further in ungodliness” — ἀσέβεια from the godliness family: irreverence. προκόπτω is the word for ‘progress’ (1 Timothy 4:15); empty talk makes progress — in the wrong direction.","words":[{"strongs":"G952","hebrew":"βέβηλος","translit":"bébēlos","english":"profane"},{"strongs":"G2757","hebrew":"κενοφωνία","translit":"kenophōnía","english":"empty chatter"},{"strongs":"G4026","hebrew":"περιΐστημι","translit":"periḯstēmi","english":"shun"},{"strongs":"G4298","hebrew":"προκόπτω","translit":"prokóptō","english":"go further"},{"strongs":"G763","hebrew":"ἀσέβεια","translit":"asébeia","english":"ungodliness"}]},{"v":17,"text":"and those words will consume like gangrene, of whom is Hymenaeus and Philetus;","understanding":"καὶ ὁ λόγος αὐτῶν ὡς γάγγραινα νομὴν ἕξει, “and those words will consume like gangrene” — γάγγραινα, a spreading ulcer that eats living flesh; νομή, spreading, feeding. False teaching does not stay contained; it spreads and destroys healthy tissue. Two examples: Ὑμέναιος, Hymenaeus (1 Timothy 1:20), and Φίλητος, Philetus.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G1044","hebrew":"γάγγραινα","translit":"gángraina","english":"gangrene"},{"strongs":"G3542","hebrew":"νομή","translit":"nomḗ","english":"consume"},{"strongs":"G5211","hebrew":"Ὑμεναῖος","translit":"Hymenaîos","english":"Hymenaeus"},{"strongs":"G5372","hebrew":"Φίλητος","translit":"Phílētos","english":"Philetus"}]},{"v":18,"text":"men who have erred concerning the truth, saying that the resurrection is already past, and overthrowing the faith of some.","understanding":"οἵτινες περὶ τὴν ἀλήθειαν ἠστόχησαν, “men who have erred concerning the truth” — ἀστοχέω, missed the mark (1 Timothy 1:6). Their error: λέγοντες ἀνάστασιν ἤδη γεγονέναι, “saying that the resurrection is already past” — ἀνάστασις from the rise family. Perhaps they taught that resurrection was only spiritual, already experienced at conversion, denying a future bodily resurrection (compare 1 Corinthians 15:12). The result: ἀνατρέπουσιν τήν τινων πίστιν, “overthrowing the faith of some” — turning it upside down.","words":[{"strongs":"G795","hebrew":"ἀστοχέω","translit":"astochéō","english":"erred"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G396","hebrew":"ἀνατρέπω","translit":"anatrépō","english":"overthrowing"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":19,"text":"However God’s firm foundation stands, having this seal, “The Lord knows those who are his,” and, “Let every one who names the name of the Lord depart from unrighteousness.”","understanding":"“However God’s firm (στερεός, solid) foundation (θεμέλιος) stands (ἕστηκεν).” Some faith is overturned, but God's foundation is not. It bears a σφραγίς, “seal,” with two inscriptions. First: “The Lord knows those who are his” (v. 19) — ἔγνω, from the know family's γινώσκω: relational knowledge, as in John 10:14, ‘I know my own’ — echoing Numbers 16:5. Second: “Let every one who names (ὀνομάζων) the name of the Lord depart (ἀποστήτω) from unrighteousness (ἀδικίας)” — ἀδικία from the sin family. God's side: He knows His own. Our side: we turn from sin.","words":[{"strongs":"G4731","hebrew":"στερεός","translit":"stereós","english":"firm"},{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stands"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G3687","hebrew":"ὀνομάζω","translit":"onomázō","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G868","hebrew":"ἀφίστημι","translit":"aphístēmi","english":"depart"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"unrighteousness"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Now in a large house there are not only vessels of gold and of silver, but also of wood and of clay. Some are for honor, and some for dishonor.","understanding":"ἐν μεγάλῃ δὲ οἰκίᾳ, “Now in a large house” — the visible church, or the world — there are σκεύη, “vessels,” χρυσᾶ καὶ ἀργυρᾶ, “of gold and of silver,” ξύλινα καὶ ὀστράκινα, “of wood and of clay.” Some are εἰς τιμήν, “for honor” (used for guests and celebrations), some εἰς ἀτιμίαν, “for dishonor” (used for waste and rubbish). In context, the dishonourable vessels are the false teachers.","words":[{"strongs":"G3614","hebrew":"οἰκία","translit":"oikía","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G4632","hebrew":"σκεῦος","translit":"skeûos","english":"vessels"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G693","hebrew":"ἀργύρεος","translit":"argýreos","english":"silver"},{"strongs":"G3585","hebrew":"ξύλινος","translit":"xýlinos","english":"wood"},{"strongs":"G3749","hebrew":"ὀστράκινος","translit":"ostrákinos","english":"clay"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G819","hebrew":"ἀτιμία","translit":"atimía","english":"dishonor"}]},{"v":21,"text":"If anyone therefore purges himself from these, he will be a vessel for honor, sanctified, and suitable for the master’s use, prepared for every good work.","understanding":"ἐὰν οὖν τις ἐκκαθάρῃ ἑαυτὸν ἀπὸ τούτων, “If anyone therefore purges himself from these” — cleanses thoroughly from these influences — he will be σκεῦος εἰς τιμήν, “a vessel for honor,” ἡγιασμένον, “sanctified,” set apart; εὔχρηστον τῷ δεσπότῃ, “suitable for the master’s use” — useful, ready to hand; εἰς πᾶν ἔργον ἀγαθὸν ἡτοιμασμένον, “prepared for every good work” — ἀγαθός from the good family. What a vessel is made of matters less than whether it is clean.","words":[{"strongs":"G1571","hebrew":"ἐκκαθαίρω","translit":"ekkathaírō","english":"purges"},{"strongs":"G4632","hebrew":"σκεῦος","translit":"skeûos","english":"vessel"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctified"},{"strongs":"G2173","hebrew":"εὔχρηστος","translit":"eúchrēstos","english":"suitable"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"master"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Flee from youthful lusts; but pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace with those who call on the Lord out of a pure heart.","understanding":"τὰς δὲ νεωτερικὰς ἐπιθυμίας φεῦγε, “Flee from youthful lusts” — not only sexual desire but the passions typical of youth: impatience, love of novelty, pride, argumentativeness. δίωκε δέ, “but pursue” — flee and pursue, as in 1 Timothy 6:11 — δικαιοσύνην, πίστιν, ἀγάπην, εἰρήνην, “righteousness, faith, love, and peace,” and do it in company: “with those who call on the Lord out of a pure (καθαρᾶς) heart.” Godliness is pursued together.","words":[{"strongs":"G3512","hebrew":"νεωτερικός","translit":"neōterikós","english":"youthful"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"Flee"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"pursue"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G1941","hebrew":"ἐπικαλέομαι","translit":"epikaléomai","english":"call on"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"}]},{"v":23,"text":"But refuse foolish and ignorant questionings, knowing that they generate strife.","understanding":"τὰς δὲ μωρὰς καὶ ἀπαιδεύτους ζητήσεις παραιτοῦ, “But refuse foolish and ignorant questionings” — μωρός, foolish (the root of ‘moron’); ἀπαίδευτος, uninstructed. εἰδὼς ὅτι γεννῶσι μάχας, “knowing that they generate strife” — μάχη, battles. Some questions are asked not to learn but to fight; they give birth to quarrels.","words":[{"strongs":"G3474","hebrew":"μωρός","translit":"mōrós","english":"foolish"},{"strongs":"G521","hebrew":"ἀπαίδευτος","translit":"apaídeutos","english":"ignorant"},{"strongs":"G2214","hebrew":"ζήτησις","translit":"zḗtēsis","english":"questionings"},{"strongs":"G3868","hebrew":"παραιτέομαι","translit":"paraitéomai","english":"refuse"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"generate"},{"strongs":"G3163","hebrew":"μάχη","translit":"máchē","english":"strife"}]},{"v":24,"text":"The Lord’s servant must not quarrel, but be gentle towards all, able to teach, patient,","understanding":"δοῦλον δὲ κυρίου οὐ δεῖ μάχεσθαι, “The Lord’s servant must not quarrel” — δοῦλος from the servant family: a bondservant who belongs wholly to the Lord. Instead: ἤπιον εἶναι πρὸς πάντας, “gentle towards all” (v. 24) — kind, mild (as in 1 Thessalonians 2:7); διδακτικόν, “able to teach” (1 Timothy 3:2); ἀνεξίκακον, “patient” — literally ‘enduring evil’, putting up with wrong without resentment.","words":[{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G3164","hebrew":"μάχομαι","translit":"máchomai","english":"quarrel"},{"strongs":"G2261","hebrew":"ἤπιος","translit":"ḗpios","english":"gentle"},{"strongs":"G1317","hebrew":"διδακτικός","translit":"didaktikós","english":"able to teach"},{"strongs":"G420","hebrew":"ἀνεξίκακος","translit":"anexíkakos","english":"patient"}]},{"v":25,"text":"in gentleness correcting those who oppose him: perhaps God may give them repentance leading to a full knowledge of the truth,","understanding":"ἐν πρᾳΰτητι παιδεύοντα τοὺς ἀντιδιατιθεμένους, “in gentleness correcting those who oppose him” — παιδεύω, to instruct as a parent trains a child. The goal: μήποτε δῴη αὐτοῖς ὁ θεὸς μετάνοιαν, “perhaps God may give them repentance” — repentance is God's gift — εἰς ἐπίγνωσιν ἀληθείας, “leading to a full knowledge of the truth” — ἐπίγνωσις from the know family: full, transforming knowledge. The aim of correction is never to win the argument but to win the person.","words":[{"strongs":"G4236","hebrew":"πρᾳότης","translit":"praiótēs","english":"gentleness"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"correcting"},{"strongs":"G475","hebrew":"ἀντιδιατίθεμαι","translit":"antidiatíthemai","english":"oppose"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G3341","hebrew":"μετάνοια","translit":"metánoia","english":"repentance"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"full knowledge"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":26,"text":"and they may recover themselves out of the devil’s snare, having been taken captive by him to his will.","understanding":"καὶ ἀνανήψωσιν, “and they may recover themselves” — literally ‘become sober again’, wake up from spiritual drunkenness — ἐκ τῆς τοῦ διαβόλου παγίδος, “out of the devil’s snare,” ἐζωγρημένοι ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “having been taken captive by him” — ζωγρέω, to capture alive — εἰς τὸ ἐκείνου θέλημα, “to his will.” Behind false teachers stands a captor. They are not only enemies to be resisted but prisoners to be rescued.","words":[{"strongs":"G366","hebrew":"ἀνανήφω","translit":"ananḗphō","english":"recover"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G3803","hebrew":"παγίς","translit":"pagís","english":"snare"},{"strongs":"G2221","hebrew":"ζωγρέω","translit":"zōgréō","english":"taken captive"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Relay Race","verseRef":"v. 2","image":"Picture a relay race. The first runner, old and worn, has carried the baton for miles. His legs are failing, but his grip is firm. He reaches out to the next runner, a young man who has been waiting, and presses the baton into his hand. The young runner sets off, and ahead of him others are already waiting, hands stretched back, ready for their turn. The race is far longer than any one runner's strength. What matters is that the baton is never dropped.","meaning":"“The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (v. 2). Paul, Timothy, faithful people, others: four runners in one verse. Paul is about to finish his leg of the race. The gospel survives not through great institutions but through faithful people passing it on, one generation to the next. Someone handed it to you. To whom are you passing it?"},{"title":"Soldier, Athlete, Farmer","verseRef":"v. 3-6","image":"Picture three people at dawn. A soldier tightens his belt and marches out, refusing to be distracted by the market stalls and business deals of the town he passes through; his eyes are on his commander. An athlete runs his training laps exactly as the rules require, knowing that a shortcut would disqualify him. A farmer bends over his field in the cold, breaking the soil and sowing seed, his back aching, his harvest months away. Each of them is enduring something hard for something better.","meaning":"“You therefore must endure hardship, as a good soldier of Christ Jesus” (v. 3). The soldier avoids entanglements “that he may please him who enrolled him as a soldier” (v. 4). The athlete “isn’t crowned unless he has competed by the rules” (v. 5). And “The farmer who labors must be the first to get a share of the crops” (v. 6). Faithful service requires focus, integrity and hard work, and each brings its reward. “Consider what I say, and may the Lord give you understanding in all things” (v. 7)."},{"title":"The Straight Cut","verseRef":"v. 15","image":"Picture a stonemason squaring a block for a temple wall. He measures carefully, marks a line and cuts straight along it. A crooked cut would leave gaps; the wall would be weak and the building would lean. Or picture a road-builder cutting a straight path through a forest so that travellers will not get lost. Now picture a preacher with an open Bible. He can twist the text to fit his opinions, or he can cut straight, letting it say what it says, so that his hearers can walk a straight path to God.","meaning":"“Give diligence to present yourself approved by God, a workman who doesn’t need to be ashamed, properly handling the Word of Truth” (v. 15). The word ὀρθοτομέω means to cut straight. Handling Scripture accurately requires diligence: careful reading, honest interpretation, faithful application. The goal is God's approval, not human applause. Every believer who reads, studies and shares the Bible is called to be this kind of workman."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Strong in Grace, Passing It On (v. 1-2)","body":"Timothy must be strengthened by grace in Christ and entrust what he heard from Paul to faithful people who will teach others."},{"label":"Soldier, Athlete, Farmer (v. 3-7)","body":"Share in suffering like a good soldier who avoids entanglements to please his commander, an athlete who competes by the rules, and a hard-working farmer who first shares the crops; reflect, and the Lord will give understanding."},{"label":"Remember Jesus Christ (v. 8-13)","body":"Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead and descended from David; for this gospel Paul suffers in chains, but God's word is not chained; he endures for the elect's salvation; the faithful saying: dying with Him we live, enduring we reign, denying Him we are denied, faithless He remains faithful."},{"label":"An Approved Workman (v. 14-19)","body":"Avoid quarrels about words; be an approved workman who rightly handles the word of truth; avoid empty chatter that spreads like gangrene, as with Hymenaeus and Philetus who say the resurrection has already happened; yet God's firm foundation stands with its seal: the Lord knows those who are His, and those who name Him must turn from unrighteousness."},{"label":"A Vessel for Honour, a Gentle Servant (v. 20-26)","body":"In a great house there are vessels for honour and dishonour; whoever cleanses himself will be a vessel for honour, useful to the Master; flee youthful passions and pursue righteousness, faith, love and peace; avoid foolish controversies; the Lord's servant must be gentle, able to teach and patient, correcting opponents with gentleness so that God may grant them repentance and they may escape the devil's snare."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Strength, Succession and Single-Mindedness","paragraphs":["Chapter 1 ended with the painful news that many had deserted Paul. Chapter 2 begins, “You therefore, my child, be strengthened in the grace that is in Christ Jesus” (v. 1). Against the background of desertion, Timothy must stand — and his strength must come from grace. Then comes the plan for the gospel's future: “The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (v. 2). This is Paul's succession plan. Not a building, not an institution, but faithful people teaching faithful people.","Paul then gives three pictures of faithful ministry. The soldier: “You therefore must endure hardship, as a good soldier of Christ Jesus. No soldier on duty entangles himself in the affairs of life, that he may please him who enrolled him as a soldier” (vv. 3–4). The word for ‘life’ is βίος — ordinary daily business. Not everything ordinary is wrong, but a soldier on campaign must not be entangled by it. The athlete: “Also, if anyone competes in athletics, he isn’t crowned unless he has competed by the rules” (v. 5). The farmer: “The farmer who labors must be the first to get a share of the crops” (v. 6).","Together they teach endurance, integrity and hard work, each with its reward: the commander's approval, the victor's crown, the harvest. Paul does not explain every detail; he tells Timothy, “Consider what I say, and may the Lord give you understanding in all things” (v. 7). Christian understanding comes through reflection and through the Lord's gift together. Paul used the same athletic picture elsewhere: “Don’t you know that those who run in a race all run, but one receives the prize? Run like that, that you may win” (1 Corinthians 9:24)."]},{"heading":"Remember Jesus Christ","paragraphs":["In the middle of the hardship, Paul points Timothy to the centre: “Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, of the offspring of David, according to my Good News” (v. 8). The resurrection proves that suffering and death are not the end; the descent from David proves that He is the promised King. Paul suffers for this gospel “to the point of chains as a criminal. But God’s word isn’t chained” (v. 9). The authorities can imprison the preacher but not the message. Indeed, Paul's prison letters have reached more people than he could ever have reached in person.","His suffering has a purpose: “Therefore I endure all things for the chosen ones’ sake, that they also may obtain the salvation which is in Christ Jesus with eternal glory” (v. 10). God has chosen people for salvation, and Paul suffers so that the gospel will reach them. Then comes the faithful saying, probably an early Christian hymn: “For if we died with him, we will also live with him. If we endure, we will also reign with him. If we deny him, he also will deny us. If we are faithless, he remains faithful. For he can’t deny himself” (vv. 11–13).","The first two lines are promises: union with Christ in death means life; endurance means reigning with Him. The third is a warning, echoing Jesus: “But whoever denies me before men, him I will also deny before my Father who is in heaven” (Matthew 10:33). The fourth is a surprise. We expect ‘if we are faithless, He will be faithless’, but instead: “he remains faithful.” Final, deliberate denial is different from the weak, wavering faith of a struggling believer. Christ's faithfulness does not depend on ours; it rests on His own unchanging character. As Paul asked, “Will their lack of faith nullify the faithfulness of God?” (Romans 3:3)."]},{"heading":"Rightly Handling the Word","paragraphs":["Timothy must remind the believers of these truths and warn them not to “argue about words, to no profit, to the subverting of those who hear” (v. 14). Instead, he must focus on his own work: “Give diligence to present yourself approved by God, a workman who doesn’t need to be ashamed, properly handling the Word of Truth” (v. 15). The picture is of cutting straight. False teachers twisted Scripture; Timothy must handle it honestly and accurately. The approval that matters is God's.","False teaching spreads like disease: “But shun empty chatter, for it will go further in ungodliness, and those words will consume like gangrene” (vv. 16–17). Hymenaeus and Philetus had “erred concerning the truth, saying that the resurrection is already past, and overthrowing the faith of some” (v. 18). They probably taught that resurrection was merely spiritual and had already happened, denying the future bodily resurrection that Paul defended in 1 Corinthians 15. Yet Paul is not shaken: “However God’s firm foundation stands, having this seal, ‘The Lord knows those who are his,’ and, ‘Let every one who names the name of the Lord depart from unrighteousness’” (v. 19). God knows His own; His own turn from sin.","The chapter closes with a picture and a portrait. The picture: a great house with valuable and common vessels, some for honour and some for dishonour. “If anyone therefore purges himself from these, he will be a vessel for honor, sanctified, and suitable for the master’s use, prepared for every good work” (v. 21). The portrait: “The Lord’s servant must not quarrel, but be gentle towards all, able to teach, patient, in gentleness correcting those who oppose him: perhaps God may give them repentance leading to a full knowledge of the truth” (vv. 24–25). Firm in truth, gentle in manner, hopeful for opponents — because they are captives of the devil who need to be set free (v. 26)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Union With Christ and Gentle Correction","paragraphs":["The faithful saying of verses 11–13 rests on Paul's teaching about union with Christ: “But if we died with Christ, we believe that we will also live with him” (Romans 6:8). The warning about denial echoes Jesus' own words in Matthew 10:33. And the promise that Christ remains faithful echoes Romans 3:3–4: God's faithfulness is not cancelled by human unfaithfulness — “let God be found true, but every man a liar” (Romans 3:4).","The seal ‘The Lord knows those who are his’ echoes Jesus the Good Shepherd: “I am the good shepherd. I know my own, and I’m known by my own” (John 10:14). The false teaching about the resurrection is the same error Paul confronted in Corinth: “Now if Christ is preached, that he has been raised from the dead, how do some among you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?” (1 Corinthians 15:12).","Paul's call to gentle correction matches his teaching to the Galatians: “Brothers, even if a man is caught in some fault, you who are spiritual must restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness” (Galatians 6:1). Repentance is God's gift, as Romans says: “the goodness of God leads you to repentance” (Romans 2:4). And the captivity of unbelievers is described in 2 Corinthians: “the god of this world has blinded the minds of the unbelieving” (2 Corinthians 4:4). Gentle truth-telling is one of God's means of setting captives free."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Entangle / involve","hebrew":"ἐμπλέκω","translit":"emplékō","strongs":"G1707","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to entwine, i.e. (figuratively) involve with.” The apostle Peter uses the same verb of people ‘entangled’ again in the world's defilements (2 Peter 2:20). In verse 4 it describes a soldier wrapped up in civilian business. The issue is not that the affairs of life are evil, but that they can tangle around a believer's feet and slow the mission. Ask not only ‘Is this wrong?’ but ‘Is this entangling me?’"},{"english":"Bind / chain","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","strongs":"G1210","verses":"v. 9","insight":"Strong's: “to bind (in various applications, literally or figuratively).” Paul is chained ‘as a criminal’, but ‘God's word isn't chained’. The contrast is the heart of the verse. Throughout history, governments have imprisoned preachers and banned Bibles, yet the word has kept spreading. You may feel limited by circumstances, but God's word in your mouth and life is never bound."},{"english":"Approved / tested and genuine","hebrew":"δόκιμος","translit":"dókimos","strongs":"G1384","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “properly, acceptable (current after assayal), i.e. approved.” It describes a coin that has been tested and found genuine, accepted as currency. The related verb δοκιμάζω means to test (1 Thessalonians 2:4). Timothy must aim to stand before God as a workman whose work passes inspection. The goal is not human approval or popularity but God's verdict: ‘tested and genuine’."},{"english":"Rightly handle / cut straight","hebrew":"ὀρθοτομέω","translit":"orthotoméō","strongs":"G3718","verses":"v. 15","insight":"This word appears only here in the New Testament. It combines ὀρθός, straight, and τέμνω, to cut. In the Greek Old Testament it is used of God making straight paths (Proverbs 3:6; 11:5). It may picture a road-builder cutting a straight road, a mason cutting stone true, or a farmer ploughing a straight furrow. Handling God's word rightly means teaching it accurately, without twisting it, so that people can walk straight in it."},{"english":"Patient under wrong / forbearing","hebrew":"ἀνεξίκακος","translit":"anexíkakos","strongs":"G420","verses":"v. 24","insight":"Strong's: “enduring of ill, i.e. forbearing.” It appears only here in the New Testament. It combines ἀνέχομαι, to bear with, and κακός, evil: one who bears wrong without becoming bitter. The Lord's servant will be wronged, misunderstood and opposed. Instead of fighting back, he bears it patiently, correcting gently in the hope that God will grant repentance."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: who you are passing the faith to, what is entangling you, and how you handle those who oppose you.","items":[{"title":"Who Is Your Faithful Person?","body":"Paul's succession plan was simple: entrust the gospel to faithful people who will teach others. The Spirit asks: who is your ‘faithful person’? Is there someone younger in faith you are investing in, teaching, encouraging and walking with? If not, ask Him to show you one person. The gospel moves forward one faithful life at a time."},{"title":"What Has Entangled You?","body":"A soldier on duty does not get tangled in civilian affairs. The Spirit may be showing you something good in itself — a hobby, a business opportunity, entertainment, a relationship, a busy schedule — that has wrapped itself around your feet and slowed your obedience. Ask Him: ‘What is entangling me?’ Then decide whether to cut it loose or put it in its proper place."},{"title":"Gentle With Opponents","body":"The Lord's servant must not quarrel but be gentle, patient and hopeful toward opponents. The Spirit may be convicting you about a harsh tone, an argumentative spirit, or a desire to win debates rather than people. Remember that opponents may be captives of the enemy. Ask the Spirit to give you gentleness, and pray that God will grant them repentance."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Relay Check","body":"Write down who passed the faith to you, and who you are passing it to (v. 2). If the second line is empty, ask God for one name this week."},{"title":"The Entanglement Check","body":"List the main activities that take your time and energy. Circle any that entangle you and distract you from pleasing Christ (v. 4). Choose one to limit."},{"title":"The Handling Check","body":"When you share Scripture, do you handle it accurately and in context (v. 15)? Or do you sometimes use verses to support what you already wanted to say? Ask God to make you an honest workman with His word."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Start a Relay","body":"Choose one person who is newer in faith and invite them to meet with you regularly for the next three months — weekly or fortnightly. Read a book of the Bible together (2 Timothy is ideal), share what you are learning, and pray together. Encourage them, from the beginning, to think about who they will one day pass the faith on to. Paul's plan works one person at a time."},{"title":"Cut It Straight","body":"Practise ‘rightly handling the word of truth’ this week. Take one passage — for example 2 Timothy 2:1–13 — and study it carefully: read it several times, note the context, look up key words, ask what it meant to Timothy before asking what it means for you. Write a short summary in your own words. Then share one true, accurate insight from it with someone else."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, strengthen me in the grace that is in You. Help me pass on what I have received to faithful people who will teach others also. Make me a good soldier who endures hardship and is not entangled in the affairs of life, so that I may please You who enlisted me. Make me an athlete who competes by the rules, and a hard-working farmer who waits patiently for the harvest. I remember You, Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, descended from David. Thank You that Your word is not chained. Help me endure everything for the sake of those You have chosen. If I have died with You, I will live with You; if I endure, I will reign with You; keep me from ever denying You; and thank You that even when I am faithless, You remain faithful, because You cannot deny Yourself. Make me a workman approved by God, who handles Your word of truth accurately. Keep me from quarrels about words and empty chatter. Cleanse me, so that I may be a vessel for honour, set apart and useful to You, ready for every good work. Help me flee youthful passions and pursue righteousness, faith, love and peace. Make me gentle, patient and able to teach, correcting others with gentleness, hoping that You will grant them repentance and set them free. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Elijah Passes the Mantle to Elisha","ref":"2 Kings 2:1-15","relatedVerses":"v. 1-2","summary":"As the time came for Elijah to be taken up to heaven, his servant Elisha refused to leave him, following him from Gilgal to Bethel to Jericho to the Jordan. Elijah struck the river with his cloak and they crossed on dry ground. Elisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit. A chariot of fire appeared, and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven. Elisha picked up Elijah's fallen mantle, struck the Jordan and cried, ‘Where is the LORD, the God of Elijah?’ The waters parted again, and the prophets watching said that the spirit of Elijah rested on Elisha. Like Paul handing the deposit to Timothy, Elijah passed his ministry to the next generation — and God's power continued."},{"title":"Peter Denies and Is Restored","ref":"John 21:15-19","relatedVerses":"v. 12-13","summary":"After Peter denied Jesus three times on the night of His arrest, the risen Lord met him on the shore of the Sea of Galilee. Three times Jesus asked Peter whether he loved Him, and three times Peter answered that he did. Each time Jesus gave him a commission: feed My lambs, tend My sheep, feed My sheep. Then He told Peter how he would one day glorify God by his death, and said, ‘Follow Me.’ Peter had been faithless, but Christ remained faithful. This is the hope of 2 Timothy 2:13: our failures do not cancel Christ's faithfulness, and He restores those who return to Him."},{"title":"The Bereans Handle the Word Rightly","ref":"Acts 17:10-12","relatedVerses":"v. 15","summary":"When Paul and Silas came to Berea after fleeing Thessalonica, they preached in the synagogue. The Bereans were more noble-minded than those in Thessalonica, because they received the message with great eagerness and examined the Scriptures every day to see whether what Paul said was true. As a result, many of them believed. The Bereans show what it means to handle the word of truth rightly: eager to hear, careful to check, ready to obey. Every believer can be this kind of workman."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Timothy","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Grievous Times and the God-Breathed Word","themeTitle":"“Every Scripture Is God-Breathed”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"But know this, that in the last days, grievous times will come.","understanding":"τοῦτο δὲ γίνωσκε, “But know this” — γινώσκω from the know family: understand it, take it in — ὅτι ἐν ἐσχάταις ἡμέραις, “that in the last days.” In the New Testament the ‘last days’ began with Christ's first coming (Acts 2:17; Hebrews 1:2) and run until His return; Paul is describing Timothy's own time as well as the future. ἐνστήσονται καιροὶ χαλεποί, “grievous times will come” — καιρός from the time family: seasons with a particular character; χαλεπός, hard, dangerous, savage (used of the violent demoniacs in Matthew 8:28).","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G1764","hebrew":"ἐνίστημι","translit":"enístēmi","english":"will come"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"times"},{"strongs":"G5467","hebrew":"χαλεπός","translit":"chalepós","english":"grievous"}]},{"v":2,"text":"For men will be lovers of self, lovers of money, boastful, arrogant, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, unthankful, unholy,","understanding":"The list begins and ends with love gone wrong. ἔσονται γὰρ οἱ ἄνθρωποι φίλαυτοι, “For men will be lovers of self” — love curved inward. φιλάργυροι, “lovers of money” — the very root of evil that Paul had already warned Timothy about in his first letter (1 Timothy 6:10). Then ἀλαζόνες, “boastful”; ὑπερήφανοι, “arrogant” — showing oneself above others; βλάσφημοι, “blasphemers”; γονεῦσιν ἀπειθεῖς, “disobedient to parents”; ἀχάριστοι, “unthankful”; ἀνόσιοι, “unholy.” When self is the centre of love, every relationship is poisoned: with God, parents and others.","words":[{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G5367","hebrew":"φίλαυτος","translit":"phílautos","english":"lovers of self"},{"strongs":"G5366","hebrew":"φιλάργυρος","translit":"philárgyros","english":"lovers of money"},{"strongs":"G213","hebrew":"ἀλαζών","translit":"alazṓn","english":"boastful"},{"strongs":"G5244","hebrew":"ὑπερήφανος","translit":"hyperḗphanos","english":"arrogant"},{"strongs":"G989","hebrew":"βλάσφημος","translit":"blásphēmos","english":"blasphemers"},{"strongs":"G1118","hebrew":"γονεύς","translit":"goneús","english":"parents"},{"strongs":"G545","hebrew":"ἀπειθής","translit":"apeithḗs","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G884","hebrew":"ἀχάριστος","translit":"acháristos","english":"unthankful"},{"strongs":"G462","hebrew":"ἀνόσιος","translit":"anósios","english":"unholy"}]},{"v":3,"text":"without natural affection, unforgiving, slanderers, without self-control, fierce, not lovers of good,","understanding":"ἄστοργοι, “without natural affection” — lacking στοργή, the family love between parents and children (the opposite of φιλόστοργος in Romans 12:10); ἄσπονδοι, “unforgiving” — literally ‘without a libation’, refusing any truce; διάβολοι, “slanderers” — the devil's own name (1 Timothy 3:11); ἀκρατεῖς, “without self-control”; ἀνήμεροι, “fierce” — untamed, savage; ἀφιλάγαθοι, “not lovers of good” — another love-family compound: love withdrawn from goodness itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G794","hebrew":"ἄστοργος","translit":"ástorgos","english":"without natural affection"},{"strongs":"G786","hebrew":"ἄσπονδος","translit":"áspondos","english":"unforgiving"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"slanderers"},{"strongs":"G193","hebrew":"ἀκρατής","translit":"akratḗs","english":"without self-control"},{"strongs":"G434","hebrew":"ἀνήμερος","translit":"anḗmeros","english":"fierce"},{"strongs":"G865","hebrew":"ἀφιλάγαθος","translit":"aphilágathos","english":"not lovers of good"}]},{"v":4,"text":"traitors, headstrong, conceited, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God;","understanding":"προδόται, “traitors” — betrayers; προπετεῖς, “headstrong” — falling forward, reckless; τετυφωμένοι, “conceited” — wrapped in smoke (1 Timothy 3:6; 6:4). And the summary: φιλήδονοι μᾶλλον ἢ φιλόθεοι, “lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God.” Two more love-family compounds, set against each other. The problem of the last days is not a lack of love but a misdirection of it: self, money and pleasure loved in God's place.","words":[{"strongs":"G4273","hebrew":"προδότης","translit":"prodótēs","english":"traitors"},{"strongs":"G4312","hebrew":"προπετής","translit":"propetḗs","english":"headstrong"},{"strongs":"G5187","hebrew":"τυφόω","translit":"typhóō","english":"conceited"},{"strongs":"G5369","hebrew":"φιλήδονος","translit":"philḗdonos","english":"lovers of pleasure"},{"strongs":"G5377","hebrew":"φιλόθεος","translit":"philótheos","english":"lovers of God"}]},{"v":5,"text":"holding a form of godliness, but having denied its power. Turn away from these, also.","understanding":"Two word families in one line: ἔχοντες μόρφωσιν εὐσεβείας, “holding a form of godliness” — μόρφωσις is an outward shape, an appearance; εὐσέβεια, the key word of the pastoral letters. τὴν δὲ δύναμιν αὐτῆς ἠρνημένοι, “but having denied its power” — δύναμις from the power family: the inherent power that makes godliness real and transforming. These people are religious on the outside, but they reject the power that changes lives. καὶ τούτους ἀποτρέπου, “Turn away from these, also.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3446","hebrew":"μόρφωσις","translit":"mórphōsis","english":"form"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"denied"},{"strongs":"G665","hebrew":"ἀποτρέπω","translit":"apotrépō","english":"Turn away"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For some of these are people who creep into houses, and take captive gullible women loaded down with sins, led away by various lusts,","understanding":"Some of them ἐνδύνοντες εἰς τὰς οἰκίας, “creep into houses” — worm their way in — καὶ αἰχμαλωτεύοντες, “and take captive” — prisoners of war — γυναικάρια, “gullible women” — literally ‘little women’, a word of contempt for the false teachers' targets, not for women in general. These particular people were σεσωρευμένα ἁμαρτίαις, “loaded down with sins” — weighed down with guilt — ἀγόμενα ἐπιθυμίαις ποικίλαις, “led away by various lusts.” False teachers prey on burdened consciences and restless desires.","words":[{"strongs":"G1744","hebrew":"ἐνδύνω","translit":"endýnō","english":"creep"},{"strongs":"G3614","hebrew":"οἰκία","translit":"oikía","english":"houses"},{"strongs":"G162","hebrew":"αἰχμαλωτεύω","translit":"aichmalōteúō","english":"take captive"},{"strongs":"G1133","hebrew":"γυναικάριον","translit":"gynaikárion","english":"gullible women"},{"strongs":"G4987","hebrew":"σωρεύω","translit":"sōreúō","english":"loaded down"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G71","hebrew":"ἄγω","translit":"ágō","english":"led away"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G4164","hebrew":"ποικίλος","translit":"poikílos","english":"various"}]},{"v":7,"text":"always learning, and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth.","understanding":"πάντοτε μανθάνοντα, “always learning” — always taking in new ideas — καὶ μηδέποτε εἰς ἐπίγνωσιν ἀληθείας ἐλθεῖν δυνάμενα, “and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth” — ἐπίγνωσις from the know family: full, transforming knowledge. Endless learning without arriving at the truth is a tragedy. Information is not transformation.","words":[{"strongs":"G3842","hebrew":"πάντοτε","translit":"pántote","english":"always"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learning"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Even as Jannes and Jambres opposed Moses, so do these also oppose the truth; men corrupted in mind, who concerning the faith are rejected.","understanding":"ὃν τρόπον δὲ Ἰάννης καὶ Ἰαμβρῆς ἀντέστησαν Μωϋσεῖ, “Even as Jannes and Jambres opposed Moses.” These names are not in the Old Testament; Jewish tradition gave them to Pharaoh's magicians, who copied Moses' signs (Exodus 7:11, 22). Paul uses the familiar names. So these teachers ἀνθίστανται τῇ ἀληθείᾳ, “oppose the truth” — κατεφθαρμένοι τὸν νοῦν, “men corrupted in mind,” ἀδόκιμοι περὶ τὴν πίστιν, “who concerning the faith are rejected” — failed the test, like counterfeit coins (the opposite of δόκιμος, 2:15).","words":[{"strongs":"G2389","hebrew":"Ἰαννῆς","translit":"Iannēs","english":"Jannes"},{"strongs":"G2387","hebrew":"Ἰαμβρῆς","translit":"Iambrēs","english":"Jambres"},{"strongs":"G436","hebrew":"ἀνθίστημι","translit":"anthístēmi","english":"opposed"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G2704","hebrew":"καταφθείρω","translit":"kataphtheírō","english":"corrupted"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G96","hebrew":"ἀδόκιμος","translit":"adókimos","english":"rejected"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But they will proceed no further. For their folly will be evident to all men, as theirs also came to be.","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ οὐ προκόψουσιν ἐπὶ πλεῖον, “But they will proceed no further” — their ‘progress’ (2:16) has a limit. ἡ γὰρ ἄνοια αὐτῶν ἔκδηλος ἔσται πᾶσιν, “For their folly will be evident to all men,” ὡς καὶ ἡ ἐκείνων ἐγένετο, “as theirs also came to be.” Pharaoh's magicians eventually could not copy God's signs and admitted, ‘This is the finger of God’ (Exodus 8:19). Error eventually exposes itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G4298","hebrew":"προκόπτω","translit":"prokóptō","english":"proceed"},{"strongs":"G454","hebrew":"ἄνοια","translit":"ánoia","english":"folly"},{"strongs":"G1552","hebrew":"ἔκδηλος","translit":"ékdēlos","english":"evident"}]},{"v":10,"text":"But you did follow my teaching, conduct, purpose, faith, patience, love, steadfastness,","understanding":"σὺ δὲ παρηκολούθησάς μου, “But you did follow” — closely followed, like a disciple walking behind a teacher — nine things: τῇ διδασκαλίᾳ, “teaching”; τῇ ἀγωγῇ, “conduct” — way of life; τῇ προθέσει, “purpose”; τῇ πίστει, “faith”; τῇ μακροθυμίᾳ, “patience” — long-tempered endurance of people; τῇ ἀγάπῃ, “love”; τῇ ὑπομονῇ, “steadfastness” — endurance under circumstances. Timothy had seen Paul's whole life, not only his sermons.","words":[{"strongs":"G3877","hebrew":"παρακολουθέω","translit":"parakolouthéō","english":"follow"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G72","hebrew":"ἀγωγή","translit":"agōgḗ","english":"conduct"},{"strongs":"G4286","hebrew":"πρόθεσις","translit":"próthesis","english":"purpose"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"patience"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"steadfastness"}]},{"v":11,"text":"persecutions, and sufferings: those things that happened to me at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra. I endured those persecutions. The Lord delivered me out of them all.","understanding":"τοῖς διωγμοῖς, τοῖς παθήμασιν, “persecutions, and sufferings” — and he names them: ἐν Ἀντιοχείᾳ, ἐν Ἰκονίῳ, ἐν Λύστροις, “at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra” — Timothy's own region, where Paul was driven out, threatened and stoned (Acts 13:50; 14:5, 19). Timothy may have watched Paul stoned at Lystra. οἵους διωγμοὺς ὑπήνεγκα, “I endured those persecutions” — bore them from underneath. καὶ ἐκ πάντων με ἐρρύσατο ὁ κύριος, “The Lord delivered me out of them all” — not from facing them, but through them.","words":[{"strongs":"G1375","hebrew":"διωγμός","translit":"diōgmós","english":"persecutions"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"},{"strongs":"G490","hebrew":"Ἀντιόχεια","translit":"Antiócheia","english":"Antioch"},{"strongs":"G2430","hebrew":"Ἰκόνιον","translit":"Ikónion","english":"Iconium"},{"strongs":"G3082","hebrew":"Λύστρα","translit":"Lýstra","english":"Lystra"},{"strongs":"G5297","hebrew":"ὑποφέρω","translit":"hypophérō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G4506","hebrew":"ῥύομαι","translit":"rhýomai","english":"delivered"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Yes, and all who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus will suffer persecution.","understanding":"καὶ πάντες δὲ οἱ θέλοντες εὐσεβῶς ζῆν ἐν Χριστῷ Ἰησοῦ διωχθήσονται, “Yes, and all who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus will suffer persecution.” εὐσεβῶς from the godliness family: living reverently. Persecution is not a sign of failure but a normal result of godliness in a fallen world. Jesus said, “If they persecuted me, they will also persecute you” (John 15:20). The promise is universal: ‘all’.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G2153","hebrew":"εὐσεβῶς","translit":"eusebōs","english":"godly"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"suffer persecution"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But evil men and impostors will grow worse and worse, deceiving and being deceived.","understanding":"πονηροὶ δὲ ἄνθρωποι καὶ γόητες, “But evil men and impostors” — πονηρός from the evil family: actively harmful; γόης, originally a sorcerer muttering spells, a swindler — προκόψουσιν ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον, “will grow worse and worse” — make ‘progress’ toward the worse. πλανῶντες καὶ πλανώμενοι, “deceiving and being deceived” — deceivers are themselves deceived. Error enslaves those who spread it.","words":[{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G1114","hebrew":"γόης","translit":"góēs","english":"impostors"},{"strongs":"G4298","hebrew":"προκόπτω","translit":"prokóptō","english":"grow"},{"strongs":"G5501","hebrew":"χείρων","translit":"cheírōn","english":"worse"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceiving"}]},{"v":14,"text":"But you remain in the things which you have learned and have been assured of, knowing from whom you have learned them.","understanding":"σὺ δὲ μένε, “But you remain” — stay put, abide — ἐν οἷς ἔμαθες καὶ ἐπιστώθης, “in the things which you have learned and have been assured of” — πιστόω, made sure, convinced. εἰδὼς παρὰ τίνων ἔμαθες, “knowing from whom you have learned them” — plural: Paul, Lois, Eunice. The character of those who taught you is part of the evidence for what they taught. Others drift; Timothy must stay.","words":[{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remain"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learned"},{"strongs":"G4104","hebrew":"πιστόω","translit":"pistóō","english":"assured"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"}]},{"v":15,"text":"From infancy, you have known the holy Scriptures which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith, which is in Christ Jesus.","understanding":"καὶ ὅτι ἀπὸ βρέφους, “From infancy” — βρέφος, a baby — ἱερὰ γράμματα οἶδας, “you have known the holy Scriptures” — the Old Testament, taught by his mother and grandmother. These are τὰ δυνάμενά σε σοφίσαι εἰς σωτηρίαν, “able to make you wise for salvation” — δύναμαι, the verb of the power family: they have power to make wise — διὰ πίστεως τῆς ἐν Χριστῷ Ἰησοῦ, “through faith, which is in Christ Jesus.” The Scriptures point to Christ, as Jesus said: “these are they which testify about me” (John 5:39).","words":[{"strongs":"G1025","hebrew":"βρέφος","translit":"bréphos","english":"infancy"},{"strongs":"G2413","hebrew":"ἱερός","translit":"hierós","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1121","hebrew":"γράμμα","translit":"grámma","english":"Scriptures"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"known"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G4679","hebrew":"σοφίζω","translit":"sophízō","english":"make wise"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for instruction in righteousness,","understanding":"πᾶσα γραφὴ θεόπνευστος, “Every Scripture is God-breathed.” θεόπνευστος (θεός + πνέω, to breathe) appears only here: Scripture is breathed out by God, the product of His breath, as human speech is the product of breath. Grammatically it can also be read ‘every God-breathed Scripture is profitable’, but the most natural reading, followed by the WEB, is that all Scripture is God-breathed. Because it is God's breath, it is ὠφέλιμος, “profitable,” for four things: πρὸς διδασκαλίαν, “teaching” — what is true; πρὸς ἔλεγχον, “reproof” — what is wrong; πρὸς ἐπανόρθωσιν, “correction” — how to set it right; πρὸς παιδείαν τὴν ἐν δικαιοσύνῃ, “instruction in righteousness” — how to keep walking right.","words":[{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G2315","hebrew":"θεόπνευστος","translit":"theópneustos","english":"God-breathed"},{"strongs":"G5624","hebrew":"ὠφέλιμος","translit":"ōphélimos","english":"profitable"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G1650","hebrew":"ἔλεγχος","translit":"élenchos","english":"reproof"},{"strongs":"G1882","hebrew":"ἐπανόρθωσις","translit":"epanórthōsis","english":"correction"},{"strongs":"G3809","hebrew":"παιδεία","translit":"paideía","english":"instruction"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"}]},{"v":17,"text":"that the man of God may be complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work.","understanding":"The goal: ἵνα ἄρτιος ᾖ ὁ τοῦ θεοῦ ἄνθρωπος, “that the man of God may be complete” — ἄρτιος, fitted, capable, exactly suited for the task — πρὸς πᾶν ἔργον ἀγαθὸν ἐξηρτισμένος, “thoroughly equipped for every good work” — ἐξαρτίζω, fully fitted out, like a ship supplied for a voyage. ἀγαθός from the good family. Scripture does not merely inform; it equips for doing good. No part of a believer's calling lies outside its reach.","words":[{"strongs":"G739","hebrew":"ἄρτιος","translit":"ártios","english":"complete"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1822","hebrew":"ἐξαρτίζω","translit":"exartízō","english":"thoroughly equipped"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Love Turned Inward","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture a beautiful fountain in a city square. It was designed to pour out water to everyone who passes by. But over time, someone has turned all its spouts inward, so that the water flows back into its own basin. People walk past thirsty. The fountain is full, overflowing with itself, but gives nothing away. Its outside still looks like a fountain — elegant stone, polished bronze — but it has stopped doing what fountains do.","meaning":"Paul describes the last days: “For men will be lovers of self, lovers of money … lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God; holding a form of godliness, but having denied its power” (vv. 2, 4–5). The love-family words show love itself misdirected — to self, money and pleasure instead of God. And religion can keep its outward form while losing its power. Ask the Spirit: are the spouts of my love turned inward? Is my godliness only a form?"},{"title":"Always Learning, Never Arriving","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture someone walking endlessly around a maze with a lamp. They keep turning new corners, discovering new passages, reading new signs on the walls, always excited about the next discovery. But they never reach the centre, because they are not following the map; they are following their curiosity and their cravings. Years pass, and they know a great deal about the maze, but they are still lost in it.","meaning":"Paul describes people “always learning, and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth” (v. 7). They were vulnerable to false teachers because they were weighed down with sins and led by various desires (v. 6). Learning is good, but learning that never leads to repentance, faith and obedience leaves people lost. The goal is not more information but ἐπίγνωσις — full, transforming knowledge of the truth in Christ."},{"title":"The Breath of God","verseRef":"v. 15-17","image":"Picture God bending over the dust of the ground in Eden, forming a man, and then breathing into his nostrils the breath of life. The lifeless clay becomes a living person. Now picture a scroll of Scripture in a young boy's hands in Lystra, his grandmother pointing to the words as she teaches him. The same God who breathed life into Adam has breathed out these words. As the boy grows, those words breathe life into him too — making him wise for salvation, correcting him, training him, equipping him for a lifetime of good works.","meaning":"“Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for instruction in righteousness, that the man of God may be complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work” (vv. 16–17). Scripture is not merely human reflection about God; it is God's own breath in written form. That is why it has the power to make us “wise for salvation through faith, which is in Christ Jesus” (v. 15) and to equip us completely. In grievous times, the answer is not a new message but the God-breathed one."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Grievous Times (v. 1-5)","body":"In the last days difficult times will come: people will love themselves, money and pleasure rather than God, and display many other vices, having an appearance of godliness while denying its power; Timothy must avoid such people."},{"label":"Deceivers and Their Victims (v. 6-9)","body":"Some of these worm their way into homes and capture weak, sin-burdened people who are always learning but never reach the knowledge of the truth; like Jannes and Jambres opposing Moses, they oppose the truth, but their folly will become plain to all."},{"label":"Paul's Example of Endurance (v. 10-13)","body":"Timothy has followed Paul's teaching, way of life, purpose, faith, patience, love, endurance, persecutions and sufferings at Antioch, Iconium and Lystra, from which the Lord rescued him; all who want to live godly in Christ will be persecuted, while evil people and impostors go from bad to worse."},{"label":"Continue in the God-Breathed Scriptures (v. 14-17)","body":"Timothy must continue in what he has learned, knowing from whom he learned it and that from childhood he has known the sacred writings that make one wise for salvation through faith in Christ; all Scripture is God-breathed and useful for teaching, reproof, correction and training in righteousness, so that the man of God is complete and equipped for every good work."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Love Gone Wrong","paragraphs":["Chapter 3 opens with a sober warning: “But know this, that in the last days, grievous times will come” (v. 1). The ‘last days’ in the New Testament began with Christ's coming and the outpouring of the Spirit (Acts 2:17) and continue until His return. Paul is describing conditions Timothy already faced and that would intensify. Jesus warned of the same: “Because iniquity will be multiplied, the love of many will grow cold” (Matthew 24:12).","The list of vices in verses 2–4 is framed by love. It begins with “lovers of self, lovers of money” (v. 2) and ends with “lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God” (v. 4). In between come pride, ingratitude, disobedience to parents, lack of natural affection, slander, cruelty and betrayal. The love-family compounds make the point vivid: these people are not without love; their love is misdirected. When self, money and pleasure take God's place, every other relationship is corrupted. The list closely resembles Paul's description of humanity apart from God in Romans 1:29–31.","Most striking is verse 5: “holding a form of godliness, but having denied its power.” This describes religious people, not obvious pagans. They have the outward shape of εὐσέβεια — religious language, attendance, perhaps teaching — but they deny the δύναμις that makes godliness real: the power of God that transforms the heart. Paul's instruction is blunt: “Turn away from these, also.” Not every form of religion is from God; the test is whether its power is changing lives."]},{"heading":"Deceivers, Victims and Their End","paragraphs":["Paul describes the method of the false teachers: “For some of these are people who creep into houses, and take captive gullible women loaded down with sins, led away by various lusts, always learning, and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth” (vv. 6–7). The word for ‘gullible women’ is a contemptuous term for the vulnerable people the false teachers exploited, not a statement about women in general. The real issue is spiritual: burdened consciences, restless desires and endless curiosity without commitment make people easy prey.","He compares these teachers to “Jannes and Jambres” who “opposed Moses” (v. 8) — names Jewish tradition gave to Pharaoh's magicians, who imitated Moses' miracles until they could not. Like them, these men “oppose the truth; men corrupted in mind, who concerning the faith are rejected” (v. 8). But their success is limited: “But they will proceed no further. For their folly will be evident to all men, as theirs also came to be” (v. 9). Error may spread for a time, but God sets its limits, and eventually its emptiness is exposed.","Meanwhile Timothy has a different model to follow: “But you did follow my teaching, conduct, purpose, faith, patience, love, steadfastness, persecutions, and sufferings” (vv. 10–11). He had watched Paul's whole life. He knew what happened “at Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra” (v. 11), where Paul was persecuted, threatened and stoned (Acts 14:19). And he knew the outcome: “I endured those persecutions. The Lord delivered me out of them all” (v. 11). Then comes a universal promise: “Yes, and all who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus will suffer persecution” (v. 12). Godliness provokes opposition; it always has."]},{"heading":"Every Scripture Is God-Breathed","paragraphs":["In contrast to impostors who “grow worse and worse, deceiving and being deceived” (v. 13), Timothy must stay: “But you remain in the things which you have learned and have been assured of, knowing from whom you have learned them” (v. 14). His teachers' lives — Paul, Lois, Eunice — confirmed their teaching. And the foundation was Scripture: “From infancy, you have known the holy Scriptures which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith, which is in Christ Jesus” (v. 15). The Old Testament Scriptures, rightly read, lead to Christ.","Then comes one of the most important statements about the Bible in the Bible: “Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for instruction in righteousness” (v. 16). θεόπνευστος means breathed out by God. Scripture is not merely human words about God, or human words that God later breathes into; it is God's own breath expressed in writing through human authors. Peter says the same: “holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” (2 Peter 1:21). And Peter already counted Paul's letters among ‘the other Scriptures’ (2 Peter 3:16).","Because Scripture is God-breathed, it is profitable in four ways: teaching what is true, reproving what is wrong, correcting how to make it right, and training in righteousness so that we keep walking rightly. Its goal is practical: “that the man of God may be complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work” (v. 17). In grievous times, believers do not need a new revelation, a secret knowledge or a stronger form of religion. They need to remain in the God-breathed Scriptures, which have power to make them wise for salvation and to equip them fully."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Last Days, Persecution and the Authority of Scripture","paragraphs":["The vices of verses 2–4 echo Paul's description of humanity without God in Romans: “being filled with all unrighteousness, sexual immorality, wickedness, covetousness, malice” (Romans 1:29), “backbiters, hateful to God, insolent, haughty, boastful, inventors of evil things, disobedient to parents” (Romans 1:30), and “without understanding, covenant breakers, without natural affection, unforgiving, unmerciful” (Romans 1:31). The tragedy of 2 Timothy 3 is that these marks appear among people with a form of godliness.","The promise of persecution comes from Jesus: “Remember the word that I said to you: ‘A servant is not greater than his lord.’ If they persecuted me, they will also persecute you” (John 15:20). Paul experienced it at Antioch, where opponents “stirred up a persecution against Paul and Barnabas, and threw them out of their borders” (Acts 13:50), and at Lystra, where “they stoned Paul, and dragged him out of the city, supposing that he was dead” (Acts 14:19). Afterwards he strengthened the disciples, “exhorting them to continue in the faith, and that through many afflictions we must enter into God’s Kingdom” (Acts 14:22).","The New Testament repeatedly affirms the divine origin and power of Scripture. “For no prophecy ever came by the will of man: but holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” (2 Peter 1:21). “For the word of God is living and active, and sharper than any two-edged sword” (Hebrews 4:12). “For whatever things were written before were written for our learning, that through perseverance and through encouragement of the Scriptures we might have hope” (Romans 15:4). And Jesus said of the Scriptures, “these are they which testify about me” (John 5:39)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Lover of self / selfish","hebrew":"φίλαυτος","translit":"phílautos","strongs":"G5367","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “fond of self, i.e. selfish.” It appears only here in the New Testament, at the head of the list of last-days vices. The list is framed by φιλ- compounds: lovers of self and money at the start, lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God at the end. Self-love is the root; the other vices grow from it. The cure is not self-hatred but the reordering of love, so that God is loved first and self is given away."},{"english":"Form / outward appearance","hebrew":"μόρφωσις","translit":"mórphōsis","strongs":"G3446","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “formation, i.e. (by implication), appearance (semblance or (concretely) formula).” Paul uses it only twice; the other place describes the ‘form of knowledge and of the truth’ that the law gave (Romans 2:20). Here it is the outward shape of godliness without its inner power. A form is not evil in itself — the gospel has a pattern of sound words (1:13) — but a form without power is an empty shell. The question is not whether we have religious forms, but whether God's power is at work through them."},{"english":"Lover of pleasure","hebrew":"φιλήδονος","translit":"philḗdonos","strongs":"G5369","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “fond of pleasure, i.e. voluptuous.” It combines φίλος and ἡδονή, pleasure (the root of ‘hedonism’). Paul sets it against φιλόθεος, lover of God. Pleasure itself is God's gift (1 Timothy 6:17 speaks of God giving ‘everything to enjoy’), but when pleasure is loved in place of God, it becomes an idol. The question is always which we love more."},{"english":"God-breathed / inspired","hebrew":"θεόπνευστος","translit":"theópneustos","strongs":"G2315","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “divinely breathed in.” It combines θεός, God, and πνέω, to breathe. The word does not mean that God breathed into existing human writings to make them special, but that Scripture is breathed out by God — His own speech in written form. It is the strongest statement in the Bible about its own origin. Because Scripture is God's breath, it carries God's authority, truthfulness and life-giving power."},{"english":"Thoroughly equip / fit out completely","hebrew":"ἐξαρτίζω","translit":"exartízō","strongs":"G1822","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “to finish out (time); figuratively, to equip fully (a teacher).” Its only other use is of days being completed, when Paul's stay at Tyre came to an end (Acts 21:5). It pictures a ship fully fitted for a voyage or a workman fully supplied with tools. Paired with ἄρτιος, complete or fit, it says that Scripture leaves the man of God lacking nothing needed for ‘every good work’. We do not need another source of revelation to live a God-pleasing life."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"The Spirit is dealing with three things in this chapter: what your love is fixed on, whether your godliness has power, and how deeply you are rooted in Scripture.","items":[{"title":"Where Is Your Love Pointing?","body":"The last days are marked by love turned toward self, money and pleasure rather than God. The Spirit gently asks: what do you love most? Where do your time, money and thoughts flow? Ask Him to show you any area where self, wealth or pleasure has taken God's place, and to reorder your loves so that you are a lover of God above all."},{"title":"Form or Power?","body":"It is possible to keep the form of godliness — attendance, vocabulary, habits — while denying its power. The Spirit wants to bring the power back into your faith. Is there an area of your life where you go through the motions but resist real change? Ask Him to make your godliness living and transforming, not just outward."},{"title":"Remain in the Word","body":"In grievous times Paul's answer is Scripture. The Spirit is calling you to remain in what you have learned and to soak yourself in the God-breathed word. Let it teach you, rebuke you, correct you and train you. Ask Him to give you a fresh hunger for Scripture and to equip you through it for every good work He has prepared for you."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Love Check","body":"Read verses 2–4 slowly. Circle any of these traits you recognise in yourself, especially self-love, love of money or love of pleasure. Confess them and ask God to reorder your loves."},{"title":"The Power Check","body":"List your regular religious practices. Next to each, write whether God's power is changing you through it, or whether it has become only a form (v. 5). Choose one to renew."},{"title":"The Scripture Check","body":"How much time did you spend in Scripture last week? Is it teaching, reproving, correcting and training you (v. 16)? Set a realistic goal to increase your intake this month."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Four Questions for Every Passage","body":"For the next month, use verse 16 as a guide for your Bible reading. After each passage, write answers to four questions: What does this teach me about God and His ways? What does it reprove in my life? What does it correct — how should I change? How does it train me to keep walking in righteousness? Over time, you will see how God-breathed Scripture equips you."},{"title":"Pass On the Scriptures","body":"Timothy knew the Scriptures from childhood because his grandmother and mother taught him. Think of a child or new believer in your life. This month, read Scripture with them regularly, explain it simply, and help them memorise a verse each week — perhaps 2 Timothy 3:16–17 first. You may be shaping a future Timothy."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord, I know that the last days bring grievous times. Guard my heart from loving myself, money or pleasure more than You. Keep me from pride, ingratitude, slander, cruelty and every love turned inward. Make me a lover of God above all things. Deliver me from a form of godliness that denies Your power; let Your power be at work in me, changing me from the inside. Protect me and those I love from deceivers who prey on burdened consciences and restless desires. Keep me from always learning and never coming to the knowledge of the truth. Thank You for those who taught me Your ways and for the example of faithful servants who endured persecution. If I suffer for living godly in Christ Jesus, help me endure, knowing You will deliver me. Help me remain in what I have learned. Thank You for the holy Scriptures, able to make me wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus. Thank You that every Scripture is God-breathed. Let it teach me, rebuke me, correct me and train me in righteousness, so that I may be complete, thoroughly equipped for every good work. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Pharaoh's Magicians","ref":"Exodus 7:8-8:19","relatedVerses":"v. 8-9","summary":"When Moses and Aaron came before Pharaoh, Aaron's staff became a serpent, and Pharaoh's magicians did the same by their secret arts — though Aaron's staff swallowed theirs. When Moses turned the Nile to blood, the magicians did the same, and when he brought frogs, they copied that too. But when the dust became gnats, the magicians could not reproduce it, and they told Pharaoh, ‘This is the finger of God.’ Jewish tradition named these magicians Jannes and Jambres. Paul uses them as a picture of false teachers who imitate the truth for a while but are finally exposed: “their folly will be evident to all men, as theirs also came to be” (v. 9)."},{"title":"Paul Stoned at Lystra","ref":"Acts 14:8-22","relatedVerses":"v. 10-12","summary":"At Lystra, Paul healed a man lame from birth, and the crowd tried to worship Paul and Barnabas as gods. But Jews from Antioch and Iconium arrived and turned the crowd: “they stoned Paul, and dragged him out of the city, supposing that he was dead” (Acts 14:19). As the disciples gathered around him, Paul got up and went back into the city. The next day he left for Derbe, then returned through Lystra, Iconium and Antioch, “confirming the souls of the disciples, exhorting them to continue in the faith, and that through many afflictions we must enter into God’s Kingdom” (Acts 14:22). Lystra was Timothy's home town; he may have watched this happen. It is the persecution Paul reminds him of in 2 Timothy 3:11."},{"title":"Josiah and the Book of the Law","ref":"2 Kings 22:8-23:3","relatedVerses":"v. 15-17","summary":"During repairs to the temple in King Josiah's reign, the high priest Hilkiah found the Book of the Law, which had been neglected for years. When it was read to the king, Josiah tore his clothes in grief, because he saw how far the nation had strayed from God's word. He humbled himself before the LORD, gathered all the people, read the whole book to them, and made a covenant to follow the LORD with all his heart and soul. Reformation swept the land. Josiah's story shows the power of God-breathed Scripture to rebuke, correct and train a whole nation in righteousness when it is rediscovered and obeyed."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Timothy","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Preach the Word, Finish the Course","themeTitle":"“I Have Kept the Faith”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I command you therefore before God and the Lord Jesus Christ, who will judge the living and the dead at his appearing and his Kingdom:","understanding":"Paul's last charge is solemn. διαμαρτύρομαι, “I command you therefore before God and the Lord Jesus Christ” — I testify earnestly, I put you under oath, with God and Christ as witnesses. Christ is the one “who will judge the living and the dead at his appearing and his Kingdom.” ἐπιφάνεια, ‘appearing’, is the shining forth of Christ at His return (the same word in 1:10 describes His first coming). Timothy is to minister as someone who will give account to the coming King.","words":[{"strongs":"G1263","hebrew":"διαμαρτύρομαι","translit":"diamartýromai","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2015","hebrew":"ἐπιφάνεια","translit":"epipháneia","english":"appearing"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"}]},{"v":2,"text":"preach the word; be urgent in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with all patience and teaching.","understanding":"Nine short commands follow, and the first is the heart of them: κήρυξον τὸν λόγον, “preach the word” — κηρύσσω, proclaim like a herald announcing the king's message. ἐπίστηθι, “be urgent” — stand ready, be on hand. Then a pair from the time family: εὐκαίρως ἀκαίρως, “in season and out of season.” Both are built on καιρός, the fitting moment: εὐκαίρως is ‘well-timed’, when circumstances are favourable; ἀκαίρως is ‘ill-timed’, when they are not. Timothy is not to wait for the right moment; every moment is the moment. ἔλεγξον, ἐπιτίμησον, παρακάλεσον, “reprove, rebuke, and exhort” — expose error, warn sharply, encourage — ἐν πάσῃ μακροθυμίᾳ καὶ διδαχῇ, “with all patience and teaching.” Correction without patience and teaching becomes harshness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preach"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2186","hebrew":"ἐφίστημι","translit":"ephístēmi","english":"be urgent"},{"strongs":"G2122","hebrew":"εὐκαίρως","translit":"eukaírōs","english":"in season"},{"strongs":"G171","hebrew":"ἀκαίρως","translit":"akaírōs","english":"out of season"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reprove"},{"strongs":"G2008","hebrew":"ἐπιτιμάω","translit":"epitimáō","english":"rebuke"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"patience"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For the time will come when they will not listen to the sound doctrine, but, having itching ears, will heap up for themselves teachers after their own lusts;","understanding":"ἔσται γὰρ καιρός, “For the time will come” — καιρός again from the time family: a season marked by a certain character — ὅτε τῆς ὑγιαινούσης διδασκαλίας οὐκ ἀνέξονται, “when they will not listen to the sound doctrine” — ἀνέχομαι, put up with, tolerate: they will not bear healthy teaching. Instead, κνηθόμενοι τὴν ἀκοήν, “having itching ears” — ears that want to be tickled — they ἐπισωρεύσουσιν διδασκάλους, “will heap up for themselves teachers after their own lusts.” They will choose teachers the way shoppers choose goods: by what they already want. Desire, not truth, sets the curriculum.","words":[{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G430","hebrew":"ἀνέχομαι","translit":"anéchomai","english":"listen"},{"strongs":"G5198","hebrew":"ὑγιαίνω","translit":"hygiaínō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G1319","hebrew":"διδασκαλία","translit":"didaskalía","english":"doctrine"},{"strongs":"G2833","hebrew":"κνήθω","translit":"knḗthō","english":"itching"},{"strongs":"G189","hebrew":"ἀκοή","translit":"akoḗ","english":"ears"},{"strongs":"G2002","hebrew":"ἐπισωρεύω","translit":"episōreúō","english":"heap up"},{"strongs":"G1320","hebrew":"διδάσκαλος","translit":"didáskalos","english":"teachers"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"}]},{"v":4,"text":"and will turn away their ears from the truth, and turn away to fables.","understanding":"Two ‘turnings’ with two different verbs: ἀπὸ μὲν τῆς ἀληθείας τὴν ἀκοὴν ἀποστρέψουσιν, “and will turn away their ears from the truth” — ἀποστρέφω, turn their backs on it; ἐπὶ δὲ τοὺς μύθους ἐκτραπήσονται, “and turn away to fables” — ἐκτρέπω, swerve aside, the medical word for a dislocated limb (1 Timothy 1:6; 5:15). μῦθος is a made-up story. People never simply leave the truth; they always turn toward something else.","words":[{"strongs":"G654","hebrew":"ἀποστρέφω","translit":"apostréphō","english":"turn away"},{"strongs":"G189","hebrew":"ἀκοή","translit":"akoḗ","english":"ears"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G1624","hebrew":"ἐκτρέπω","translit":"ektrépō","english":"turn away to"},{"strongs":"G3454","hebrew":"μῦθος","translit":"mŷthos","english":"fables"}]},{"v":5,"text":"But you be sober in all things, suffer hardship, do the work of an evangelist, and fulfill your ministry.","understanding":"σὺ δὲ νῆφε ἐν πᾶσιν, “But you be sober in all things” — νήφω, clear-headed, not intoxicated by novelty or panic. κακοπάθησον, “suffer hardship” — the word Paul used of his own sufferings in 2:9, and the call he has already pressed on Timothy twice before. ἔργον ποίησον εὐαγγελιστοῦ, “do the work of an evangelist” — ἔργον from the work family: the task of telling the good news to those who have not believed it. Even a pastor in a settled church must keep reaching outsiders. τὴν διακονίαν σου πληροφόρησον, “and fulfill your ministry” — carry it out fully, leave nothing undone.","words":[{"strongs":"G3525","hebrew":"νήφω","translit":"nḗphō","english":"be sober"},{"strongs":"G2553","hebrew":"κακοπαθέω","translit":"kakopathéō","english":"suffer hardship"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G2099","hebrew":"εὐαγγελιστής","translit":"euangelistḗs","english":"evangelist"},{"strongs":"G4135","hebrew":"πληροφορέω","translit":"plērophoréō","english":"fulfill"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"ministry"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For I am already being offered, and the time of my departure has come.","understanding":"Now the reason for urgency: ἐγὼ γὰρ ἤδη σπένδομαι, “For I am already being offered” — σπένδω, poured out as a drink offering. In the Old Testament sacrifices, wine was poured out beside the offering. Paul sees his coming execution as the final pouring out of a life already given to God. καὶ ὁ καιρὸς τῆς ἐμῆς ἀναλύσεως ἐφέστηκεν, “and the time of my departure has come.” καιρός from the time family: the appointed moment. ἀνάλυσις, ‘departure’, was used of a ship loosing from its moorings or soldiers striking camp. Death, for Paul, is not an end but a setting sail.","words":[{"strongs":"G4689","hebrew":"σπένδω","translit":"spéndō","english":"being offered"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G359","hebrew":"ἀνάλυσις","translit":"análysis","english":"departure"},{"strongs":"G2186","hebrew":"ἐφίστημι","translit":"ephístēmi","english":"has come"}]},{"v":7,"text":"I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith.","understanding":"Three perfect tenses — finished actions whose results stand. τὸν καλὸν ἀγῶνα ἠγώνισμαι, “I have fought the good fight” — καλός from the good family: noble, beautiful; ἀγών, a contest, an athletic struggle (the root of ‘agony’). τὸν δρόμον τετέλεκα, “I have finished the course” — the race is run to the end. τὴν πίστιν τετήρηκα, “I have kept the faith” — τηρέω, guarded it: kept faith with Christ, and kept the faith entrusted to him intact. Athlete, runner and guard: the whole letter's images meet in one verse.","words":[{"strongs":"G75","hebrew":"ἀγωνίζομαι","translit":"agōnízomai","english":"fought"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G73","hebrew":"ἀγών","translit":"agṓn","english":"fight"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"},{"strongs":"G1408","hebrew":"δρόμος","translit":"drómos","english":"course"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"kept"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"}]},{"v":8,"text":"From now on, there is stored up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day; and not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing.","understanding":"λοιπὸν ἀπόκειταί μοι ὁ τῆς δικαιοσύνης στέφανος, “From now on, there is stored up for me the crown of righteousness” — στέφανος, the victor's wreath given at the games, not a royal diadem. ὃν ἀποδώσει μοι ὁ κύριος, “which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day.” Roman judges were about to condemn Paul; the righteous Judge will reward him. Note the verb ἀποδίδωμι — ‘give’ here — which reappears in verse 14 as ‘repay’. And the crown is οὐ μόνον δὲ ἐμοί, “and not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing.” ἀγαπάω from the love family: those who have set their love on Christ's return. Hold this beside verse 10.","words":[{"strongs":"G3063","hebrew":"λοιπόν","translit":"loipón","english":"From now on"},{"strongs":"G606","hebrew":"ἀπόκειμαι","translit":"apókeimai","english":"stored up"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous judge"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"},{"strongs":"G2015","hebrew":"ἐπιφάνεια","translit":"epipháneia","english":"appearing"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Be diligent to come to me soon,","understanding":"σπούδασον ἐλθεῖν πρός με ταχέως, “Be diligent to come to me soon.” σπουδάζω, make every effort — the same word as 2:15. The great apostle who has just written of his crown is also a lonely man who wants his friend. Faith in eternity does not cancel the need for human company.","words":[{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"Be diligent"},{"strongs":"G5030","hebrew":"ταχέως","translit":"tachéōs","english":"soon"}]},{"v":10,"text":"for Demas left me, having loved this present world, and went to Thessalonica; Crescens to Galatia, and Titus to Dalmatia.","understanding":"Δημᾶς γάρ με ἐγκατέλιπεν ἀγαπήσας τὸν νῦν αἰῶνα, “for Demas left me, having loved this present world, and went to Thessalonica.” Two word families meet here. ἀγαπάω is the same verb as verse 8: there, faithful believers ‘loved his appearing’; here Demas ‘loved this present world’. The difference between them is not whether they loved, but what they loved. And ‘world’ is αἰών, not κόσμος: the present age with its values and pressures, the age that is passing. Demas had once been Paul's fellow worker; what he abandoned was Paul, and he did so out of love for the present age. Crescens and Titus, by contrast, seem to have gone on mission: “Crescens to Galatia, and Titus to Dalmatia.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1214","hebrew":"Δημᾶς","translit":"Dēmâs","english":"Demas"},{"strongs":"G1459","hebrew":"ἐγκαταλείπω","translit":"enkataleípō","english":"left"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"},{"strongs":"G3568","hebrew":"νῦν","translit":"nŷn","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2332","hebrew":"Θεσσαλονίκη","translit":"Thessaloníkē","english":"Thessalonica"},{"strongs":"G2913","hebrew":"Κρήσκης","translit":"Krḗskēs","english":"Crescens"},{"strongs":"G1053","hebrew":"Γαλατία","translit":"Galatía","english":"Galatia"},{"strongs":"G5103","hebrew":"Τίτος","translit":"Títos","english":"Titus"},{"strongs":"G1149","hebrew":"Δαλματία","translit":"Dalmatía","english":"Dalmatia"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Only Luke is with me. Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service.","understanding":"Λουκᾶς ἐστιν μόνος μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ, “Only Luke is with me” — the doctor who wrote Luke and Acts, faithful to the end. Then a remarkable sentence: Μᾶρκον ἀναλαβὼν ἄγε μετὰ σεαυτοῦ, “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service.” Years earlier Paul had refused to take Mark because he had deserted the team (Acts 15:38). Now Mark is εὔχρηστος, ‘useful’, εἰς διακονίαν, “for service.” A failure was not final; a broken relationship was restored.","words":[{"strongs":"G3065","hebrew":"Λουκᾶς","translit":"Loukâs","english":"Luke"},{"strongs":"G3138","hebrew":"Μάρκος","translit":"Márkos","english":"Mark"},{"strongs":"G2173","hebrew":"εὔχρηστος","translit":"eúchrēstos","english":"useful"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"service"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But I sent Tychicus to Ephesus.","understanding":"Τυχικὸν δὲ ἀπέστειλα εἰς Ἔφεσον, “But I sent Tychicus to Ephesus.” Tychicus was a trusted courier (he carried Ephesians and Colossians). He may have carried this letter too, and taken over Timothy's work in Ephesus so that Timothy could come to Rome. Even in prison, Paul is arranging for the churches.","words":[{"strongs":"G5190","hebrew":"Τυχικός","translit":"Tychikós","english":"Tychicus"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G2181","hebrew":"Ἔφεσος","translit":"Éphesos","english":"Ephesus"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Bring the cloak that I left at Troas with Carpus when you come, and the books, especially the parchments.","understanding":"τὸν φαιλόνην, “Bring the cloak that I left at Troas with Carpus when you come” — φελόνης, a heavy outer cloak, needed in a cold cell as winter approached (v. 21). καὶ τὰ βιβλία, μάλιστα τὰς μεμβράνας, “and the books, especially the parchments.” βιβλία are scrolls; μεμβράναι are parchment sheets. We do not know what they contained — perhaps copies of the Scriptures, perhaps Paul's own notes or documents. What we do know is that a man facing death still wanted to read. Body and mind both mattered to him.","words":[{"strongs":"G5341","hebrew":"φελόνης","translit":"phelónēs","english":"cloak"},{"strongs":"G5174","hebrew":"Τρωάς","translit":"Trōás","english":"Troas"},{"strongs":"G2591","hebrew":"Κάρπος","translit":"Kárpos","english":"Carpus"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"books"},{"strongs":"G3200","hebrew":"μεμβράνα","translit":"membrána","english":"parchments"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Alexander, the coppersmith, did much evil to me. The Lord will repay him according to his deeds,","understanding":"Ἀλέξανδρος ὁ χαλκεύς, “Alexander, the coppersmith, did much evil to me.” κακός from the evil family: harm, bad things. He may be the Alexander of 1 Timothy 1:20, though the name was common and we cannot be certain. Paul does not take revenge; he hands Alexander to God: ἀποδώσει αὐτῷ ὁ κύριος κατὰ τὰ ἔργα αὐτοῦ, “The Lord will repay him according to his deeds.” The verb is ἀποδίδωμι, the same as ‘give’ in verse 8: the same just Judge gives the crown and repays the evil. ἔργα, ‘deeds’, from the work family.","words":[{"strongs":"G223","hebrew":"Ἀλέξανδρος","translit":"Aléxandros","english":"Alexander"},{"strongs":"G5471","hebrew":"χαλκεύς","translit":"chalkeús","english":"coppersmith"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"repay"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"}]},{"v":15,"text":"of whom you also must beware; for he greatly opposed our words.","understanding":"ὃν καὶ σὺ φυλάσσου, “of whom you also must beware” — φυλάσσω, be on guard, the same word as ‘guard the deposit’ (1:14). Forgiving someone and trusting them are different things; Timothy must be careful. λίαν γὰρ ἀντέστη τοῖς ἡμετέροις λόγοις, “for he greatly opposed our words” — ἀνθίστημι, the verb used of Jannes and Jambres (3:8). His opposition was to the message, not only to Paul.","words":[{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"beware"},{"strongs":"G3029","hebrew":"λίαν","translit":"lían","english":"greatly"},{"strongs":"G436","hebrew":"ἀνθίστημι","translit":"anthístēmi","english":"opposed"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"}]},{"v":16,"text":"At my first defense, no one came to help me, but all left me. May it not be held against them.","understanding":"ἐν τῇ πρώτῃ μου ἀπολογίᾳ, “At my first defense” — most likely the preliminary hearing of this present trial in Rome, though some take it as his first imprisonment. ἀπολογία is a legal defence. οὐδείς μοι συμπαρεγένετο, “no one came to help me, but all left me.” No advocate, no witness, no friend stood with him. Yet Paul prays as Jesus and Stephen did: μὴ αὐτοῖς λογισθείη, “May it not be held against them.” λογίζομαι, credited to their account. He forgives those who failed him.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G627","hebrew":"ἀπολογία","translit":"apología","english":"defense"},{"strongs":"G4836","hebrew":"συμπαραγίνομαι","translit":"symparagínomai","english":"came to help"},{"strongs":"G1459","hebrew":"ἐγκαταλείπω","translit":"enkataleípō","english":"left"},{"strongs":"G3049","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","english":"held against"}]},{"v":17,"text":"But the Lord stood by me, and strengthened me, that through me the message might be fully proclaimed, and that all the Gentiles might hear; and I was delivered out of the mouth of the lion.","understanding":"ὁ δὲ κύριός μοι παρέστη καὶ ἐνεδυνάμωσέν με, “But the Lord stood by me, and strengthened me.” ἐνδυναμόω from the power family: to put δύναμις into someone, empower from within — the same word as 2:1 (‘be strengthened in the grace’) and as Philippians 4:13. When every human support was gone, Christ Himself stood at Paul's side. And the purpose: “that through me the message might be fully proclaimed, and that all the Gentiles might hear.” Paul's defence before the imperial court became a proclamation of the gospel at the centre of the Gentile world. καὶ ἐρρύσθην ἐκ στόματος λέοντος, “and I was delivered out of the mouth of the lion” — a picture of deadly danger, echoing the psalmist's cry to be saved from the lion's mouth (Psalm 22). It means rescue from immediate death; whether ‘the lion’ points to Nero or to Satan, the text does not say.","words":[{"strongs":"G3936","hebrew":"παρίστημι","translit":"parístēmi","english":"stood by"},{"strongs":"G1743","hebrew":"ἐνδυναμόω","translit":"endynamóō","english":"strengthened"},{"strongs":"G2782","hebrew":"κήρυγμα","translit":"kḗrygma","english":"message"},{"strongs":"G4135","hebrew":"πληροφορέω","translit":"plērophoréō","english":"fully proclaimed"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"Gentiles"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4506","hebrew":"ῥύομαι","translit":"rhýomai","english":"delivered"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lion"}]},{"v":18,"text":"And the Lord will deliver me from every evil work, and will preserve me for his heavenly Kingdom; to whom be the glory forever and ever. Amen.","understanding":"ῥύσεταί με ὁ κύριος ἀπὸ παντὸς ἔργου πονηροῦ, “And the Lord will deliver me from every evil work” — πονηρός from the evil family: actively wicked, malicious, the word of ‘the evil one’; ἔργον from the work family. Paul expects to die (v. 6), so this is not a promise of escape from execution. It is a promise that no evil scheme will destroy him: καὶ σώσει εἰς τὴν βασιλείαν αὐτοῦ τὴν ἐπουράνιον, “and will preserve me for his heavenly Kingdom.” Death will carry him into the Kingdom, not out of the Lord's care. ᾧ ἡ δόξα εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “to whom be the glory forever and ever. Amen.” ‘Forever’ is αἰών again — ‘to the ages of the ages’. Demas loved the present αἰών (v. 10); Paul gives glory to God for all the αἰῶνες to come.","words":[{"strongs":"G4506","hebrew":"ῥύομαι","translit":"rhýomai","english":"deliver"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"preserve"},{"strongs":"G2032","hebrew":"ἐπουράνιος","translit":"epouránios","english":"heavenly"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Greet Prisca and Aquila, and the house of Onesiphorus.","understanding":"ἄσπασαι Πρίσκαν καὶ Ἀκύλαν, “Greet Prisca and Aquila, and the house of Onesiphorus.” Prisca is the formal form of Priscilla; this couple worked with Paul in Corinth and Ephesus and risked their lives for him. Paul greets Onesiphorus' household rather than Onesiphorus himself (as in 1:16). Some think Onesiphorus had died, but the text does not say.","words":[{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"Greet"},{"strongs":"G4251","hebrew":"Πρίσκα","translit":"Príska","english":"Prisca"},{"strongs":"G207","hebrew":"Ἀκύλας","translit":"Akýlas","english":"Aquila"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G3683","hebrew":"Ὀνησίφορος","translit":"Onēsíphoros","english":"Onesiphorus"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Erastus remained at Corinth, but I left Trophimus at Miletus sick.","understanding":"Ἔραστος ἔμεινεν ἐν Κορίνθῳ, “Erastus remained at Corinth, but I left Trophimus at Miletus sick.” ἀσθενέω, weak, ill. Paul, through whom God had done extraordinary miracles (Acts 19:11), left a friend behind sick. Healing is God's gift to give, not a power the apostles could use at will. Faith and unanswered illness can stand side by side.","words":[{"strongs":"G2037","hebrew":"Ἔραστος","translit":"Érastos","english":"Erastus"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remained"},{"strongs":"G2882","hebrew":"Κόρινθος","translit":"Kórinthos","english":"Corinth"},{"strongs":"G5161","hebrew":"Τρόφιμος","translit":"Tróphimos","english":"Trophimus"},{"strongs":"G3399","hebrew":"Μίλητος","translit":"Mílētos","english":"Miletus"},{"strongs":"G770","hebrew":"ἀσθενέω","translit":"asthenéō","english":"sick"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Be diligent to come before winter. Eubulus salutes you, as do Pudens, Linus, Claudia, and all the brothers.","understanding":"σπούδασον πρὸ χειμῶνος ἐλθεῖν, “Be diligent to come before winter.” Ships stopped sailing in the Mediterranean winter; if Timothy delayed, he might not arrive until spring — perhaps too late. Then greetings from believers in Rome: “Eubulus salutes you, as do Pudens, Linus, Claudia, and all the brothers.” Early church tradition names a Linus as one of the first leaders of the church in Rome; he may be this man. Paul was abandoned by many, but not by all.","words":[{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"Be diligent"},{"strongs":"G5494","hebrew":"χειμών","translit":"cheimṓn","english":"winter"},{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"salutes"},{"strongs":"G2103","hebrew":"Εὔβουλος","translit":"Eúboulos","english":"Eubulus"},{"strongs":"G4227","hebrew":"Πούδης","translit":"Poúdēs","english":"Pudens"},{"strongs":"G3044","hebrew":"Λίνος","translit":"Línos","english":"Linus"},{"strongs":"G2803","hebrew":"Κλαυδία","translit":"Klaudía","english":"Claudia"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"}]},{"v":22,"text":"The Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. Grace be with you. Amen.","understanding":"ὁ κύριος μετὰ τοῦ πνεύματός σου, “The Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit” — ‘your’ is singular: a last blessing for Timothy himself. ἡ χάρις μεθ᾽ ὑμῶν, “Grace be with you. Amen.” — ‘you’ is plural: the whole church. These are Paul's last recorded words. The letter that began with grace (1:2) ends with grace. Paul's final word is not about himself, his chains or his death, but about the Lord's presence and grace.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Herald at Every Hour","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture a royal herald sent from the palace with an urgent message from the king. He stands in the town square at dawn, when the market is busy and people gather to listen. He stands there again at noon in the scorching heat, when only a few pass by. He stands there at dusk, when the crowd jeers and drifts away to a juggler across the square who tells them pleasant stories. He does not change the message to win back the crowd. He does not wait for a better moment. He simply proclaims what the king has said — patiently, clearly, faithfully — because he knows the king is coming.","meaning":"“preach the word; be urgent in season and out of season; reprove, rebuke, and exhort, with all patience and teaching” (v. 2). The two time-family words, εὐκαίρως and ἀκαίρως, cover every moment, favourable and unfavourable. Some will have “itching ears” and prefer fables (vv. 3–4). The herald's faithfulness does not depend on the audience's response, but on the King who sent him and who will judge the living and the dead (v. 1)."},{"title":"The Cup Poured Out","verseRef":"v. 6-8","image":"Picture an altar in the ancient temple. The sacrifice is laid on it, and the priest lifts a cup of wine and slowly pours it out beside the offering. The wine soaks into the ground and is gone — but it has been given entirely to God. Now picture a runner on the last stretch of a long race, bruised and exhausted, crossing the finishing line. The judge stands waiting with a wreath in his hands.","meaning":"“For I am already being offered, and the time of my departure has come” (v. 6). Paul's life is the poured-out drink offering; his death is the final drop. But he is not defeated: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (v. 7). And the Judge is waiting with “the crown of righteousness” (v. 8), not only for Paul but for all who love Christ's appearing. Ask the Spirit: is my life being poured out for God, or kept for myself?"},{"title":"Alone in the Courtroom — Yet Not Alone","verseRef":"v. 16-17","image":"Picture a vast Roman courtroom with marble columns, a raised judgment seat and rows of officials. An old prisoner in chains stands alone in the centre. He looks around for a friend, an advocate, a witness. Every bench where his supporters should sit is empty. And then, unseen by the court, Someone steps up beside him and stands at his shoulder. The prisoner straightens. His voice grows strong. He begins to speak — not only in his own defence, but about Jesus Christ — and the whole court hears the gospel.","meaning":"“At my first defense, no one came to help me, but all left me. May it not be held against them. But the Lord stood by me, and strengthened me, that through me the message might be fully proclaimed” (vv. 16–17). When every human support fails, the Lord does not. ἐνδυναμόω means He put power into Paul from within. Whatever courtroom, hospital room or lonely place you face, you are not alone."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Final Charge (v. 1-5)","body":"Before God and Christ the coming Judge, Paul charges Timothy to preach the word at all times, correcting and encouraging with patience, because people will turn from sound teaching to teachers who suit their desires; Timothy must stay clear-headed, endure hardship, evangelise and fulfil his ministry."},{"label":"Paul's Finish Line (v. 6-8)","body":"Paul's life is being poured out and his departure is near; he has fought the good fight, finished the race and kept the faith, and the crown of righteousness awaits him and all who love Christ's appearing."},{"label":"Personal Requests and Reports (v. 9-18)","body":"Paul urges Timothy to come quickly, reports who has left and who remains, asks for Mark, his cloak and his books, warns against Alexander, and recounts how all deserted him at his first defence but the Lord stood with him and will rescue him into His heavenly Kingdom."},{"label":"Final Greetings (v. 19-22)","body":"Greetings to Prisca, Aquila and Onesiphorus' household, news of Erastus and Trophimus, a plea to come before winter, greetings from Roman believers, and a closing blessing of grace."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Preach the Word, in Season and Out of Season","paragraphs":["Paul's last charge is given under the gaze of eternity: “I command you therefore before God and the Lord Jesus Christ, who will judge the living and the dead at his appearing and his Kingdom” (v. 1). Ministry is not a career but a stewardship to be reviewed by the returning King. Everything that follows must be read in that light.","The central command is short: “preach the word” (v. 2). Not opinions, not experiences, not the latest ideas, but the word — the gospel and the God-breathed Scriptures just described in 3:16–17. And it must be preached “in season and out of season” (v. 2). The two time-family adverbs, εὐκαίρως and ἀκαίρως, built on καιρός, cover favourable and unfavourable moments alike. The preacher does not wait for a convenient season. The preaching includes reproof, rebuke and encouragement, all “with all patience and teaching” (v. 2): truth spoken firmly but with long-suffering love.","Why the urgency? “For the time will come when they will not listen to the sound doctrine, but, having itching ears, will heap up for themselves teachers after their own lusts” (v. 3). People will choose teachers who tell them what they want to hear. They “will turn away their ears from the truth, and turn away to fables” (v. 4). In such times Timothy must “be sober in all things, suffer hardship, do the work of an evangelist, and fulfill your ministry” (v. 5). The answer to a culture of itching ears is not a softer message but a steadier messenger."]},{"heading":"I Have Fought the Good Fight","paragraphs":["Then Paul explains why he is handing over: “For I am already being offered, and the time of my departure has come” (v. 6). He had written years earlier to the Philippians, “Yes, and if I am poured out on the sacrifice and service of your faith, I rejoice, and rejoice with you all” (Philippians 2:17). Now the last of the drink offering is being poured. And ‘departure’ (ἀνάλυσις) pictures a ship loosing its ropes. Paul had told the Philippians he had “the desire to depart and be with Christ, which is far better” (Philippians 1:23).","He looks back: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (v. 7). Years before, he had told the Ephesian elders, “nor do I hold my life dear to myself, so that I may finish my race with joy” (Acts 20:24). Now he has. This is not pride; it is testimony to the grace that kept him. The fight was καλός, good and noble, because it was for Christ. The course was the one God set. And the faith he kept was both his personal trust and the gospel deposit entrusted to him.","And he looks forward: “From now on, there is stored up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day; and not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing” (v. 8). The Roman court is about to pronounce its verdict, but a higher court will reverse it. And the crown is not only for apostles. It is for every believer who has set their love on Christ's coming. The test is simple: do you love His appearing?"]},{"heading":"Abandoned, Yet the Lord Stood by Me","paragraphs":["The last section is intensely personal. Paul wants Timothy to come quickly (v. 9), because “Demas left me, having loved this present world” (v. 10). The love-family verb ἀγαπάω links verse 10 to verse 8: Demas loved the present age; the faithful love Christ's appearing. Two loves pull every believer. Meanwhile, “Only Luke is with me” (v. 11). Yet there is grace here too: Mark, who once deserted Paul, is now “useful to me for service” (v. 11). Paul asks for his cloak for the cold and his books for his mind (v. 13). He warns about Alexander, who “did much evil to me” (v. 14), and leaves him to the Lord's justice.","Then comes his account of the trial: “At my first defense, no one came to help me, but all left me. May it not be held against them” (v. 16). Like Jesus on the cross and Stephen under the stones, Paul forgives those who failed him. And he testifies: “But the Lord stood by me, and strengthened me, that through me the message might be fully proclaimed, and that all the Gentiles might hear” (v. 17). Even his trial became a pulpit. The Lord had promised years before that he would testify in Rome, and He kept His word.","Finally, Paul's confidence: “And the Lord will deliver me from every evil work, and will preserve me for his heavenly Kingdom” (v. 18). He does not expect to escape execution. He expects something better: that no evil will separate him from Christ, and that death will bring him safely into the Kingdom. He ends with greetings, practical news and grace (vv. 19–22). The last words of the great apostle are not a complaint but a doxology and a blessing."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Crown, the Appearing and the Faithful Presence of the Lord","paragraphs":["The ‘crown’ promised to the faithful appears across the New Testament. James writes that the one who endures “will receive the crown of life, which the Lord promised to those who love him” (James 1:12). Paul contrasts the athletes' perishable wreath: “Now they do it to receive a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible” (1 Corinthians 9:25). Peter promises elders, “When the chief Shepherd is revealed, you will receive the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away” (1 Peter 5:4). The crown is not earned by talent but given by grace to those who are faithful.","Loving Christ's appearing is the mark of the true believer. Titus describes believers as “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13). The Bible's last prayer is “Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (Revelation 22:20). Demas chose the other love, which John warns against: “Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world” (1 John 2:15), because “The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever” (1 John 2:17).","The Lord's standing beside Paul fulfils a pattern. In Jerusalem, “the Lord stood by him” (Acts 23:11) and promised, “Cheer up, Paul, for as you have testified about me at Jerusalem, so you must testify also at Rome” (Acts 23:11). In the storm an angel told him, “Don’t be afraid, Paul. You must stand before Caesar” (Acts 27:24). And Jesus Himself, deserted by His disciples, said, “Yet I am not alone, because the Father is with me” (John 16:32). Hebrews gathers the promise for all believers: “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you” (Hebrews 13:5)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"In season / opportunely","hebrew":"εὐκαίρως","translit":"eukaírōs","strongs":"G2122","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “opportunely.” It is built from εὖ, well, and καιρός, the fitting moment; its partner ἀκαίρως (G171) means ‘inopportunely’. Together they form a figure of speech meaning ‘whether the time is right or not’. The word of God is not bound to convenient seasons. This study tags both words as members of the time family, beside χρόνος, καιρός, ὥρα, αἰών and ἡμέρα."},{"english":"Pour out as a drink offering","hebrew":"σπένδω","translit":"spéndō","strongs":"G4689","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “to pour out as a libation, i.e. (figuratively) to devote (one's life or blood, as a sacrifice) (\"spend\").” It appears only twice in the New Testament, both times from Paul about his own life being poured out. In the Old Testament, drink offerings of wine accompanied the sacrifices. Paul sees his whole ministry as an offering to God, and his death as its final pouring."},{"english":"Departure","hebrew":"ἀνάλυσις","translit":"análysis","strongs":"G359","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “departure.” It comes from ἀναλύω, to unloose — used of untying a ship from its moorings, striking a tent, or a guest leaving a feast. Paul uses the verb in Philippians 1:23 for his desire to depart and be with Christ. Death for a believer is not destruction but release: the ropes are cast off, and the ship sets sail for home."},{"english":"Crown / victor's wreath","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","strongs":"G4735","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “a chaplet (as a badge of royalty, a prize in the public games or a symbol of honor generally; but more conspicuous and elaborate than the simple fillet, G1238 (διάδημα)), literally or figuratively.” In the Greek games the winner received a στέφανος of leaves that soon withered. The believer's crown is of righteousness, life and glory, and never fades. It is given by the righteous Judge to all who love His appearing."},{"english":"Appearing / manifestation","hebrew":"ἐπιφάνεια","translit":"epipháneia","strongs":"G2015","verses":"v. 1, 8","insight":"Strong's: “a manifestation, i.e. (specially) the advent of Christ (past or future).” The word was used in the ancient world of a god showing himself or of a ruler's ceremonial arrival in a city. In this letter it is used of both comings of Christ: His first appearing, which abolished death (1:10), and His future appearing in judgment and glory (4:1, 8). Believers live between the two appearings and love both."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"As Paul lays down his pen for the last time, the Holy Spirit speaks to you about faithfulness, about what you love, and about the Lord's presence when others fail.","items":[{"title":"Every Season Is Gospel Season","body":"The Spirit is not waiting for a more convenient moment, and neither should you. Some seasons will be favourable, others hostile, but the word must be spoken in both. Ask Him where you have been waiting for the ‘right time’ to speak about Christ, and for courage to speak now, with patience and love."},{"title":"What Is Your Heart's Love?","body":"Demas loved the present age; Paul loved Christ's appearing. The same verb, two directions. The Spirit gently asks: what are you living for? What would pull you away from Christ's people and mission? Ask Him to deepen your love for Christ's appearing until the passing age loses its grip on you."},{"title":"The Lord Will Stand by You","body":"There may be times when people you counted on fail you. The Spirit reminds you that the Lord stood by Paul when all deserted him, and that He will stand by you. Bring to Him any wound of abandonment. Ask for grace to forgive as Paul forgave, and for the strength that He alone puts into His people."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Season Check","body":"Think of one person you have avoided speaking to about Christ because the moment never seemed right. Write their name down and pray about speaking to them this week (v. 2)."},{"title":"The Ears Check","body":"Honestly assess the teachers and voices you listen to most. Do they tell you what you want to hear, or what is true? Remove one source that tickles your ears and add one that feeds you sound doctrine (vv. 3–4)."},{"title":"The Finish Line Check","body":"Imagine the end of your life. Could you say, ‘I have fought the good fight, finished the course, kept the faith’? Write down one area where you need to recommit to faithfulness (v. 7)."}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write Your Own Final Letter","body":"Like Paul, write a letter to someone you love as if it were your last. Tell them what truly matters, what you want them to hold on to, and how you hope they will live. Include your faith in Christ and your hope of His appearing. You do not have to send it, but you may decide to. Let the exercise clarify what you are living for now."},{"title":"Restore a Mark","body":"Paul restored Mark after a painful break. Think of someone with whom a relationship broke down, especially in ministry or church. Pray for them this week, and if it is right and safe, take one step toward reconciliation: a message, a call or an invitation. Also think of someone who failed and needs to hear that they are still useful for service; tell them."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You will judge the living and the dead at Your appearing and Your Kingdom. Help me live and serve in the light of that day. Make me ready to speak Your word in season and out of season, with patience and careful teaching. Guard me from itching ears and from teachers who only tell me what I want to hear. Keep me sober in all things, willing to suffer hardship, faithful to do the work of an evangelist and to fulfil the ministry You have given me. Like Paul, let my life be poured out as an offering to You. Give me grace to fight the good fight, finish the course and keep the faith. I set my love on Your appearing, not on this present age. Keep me from the way of Demas. Thank You that failure need not be final, as it was not for Mark. When people fail me, help me forgive as Paul forgave. Stand by me and strengthen me, as You stood by him, so that Your message is fully proclaimed through me. Deliver me from every evil work and bring me safely into Your heavenly Kingdom. To You be the glory forever and ever. Amen. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Paul's Farewell to the Ephesian Elders","ref":"Acts 20:17-38","relatedVerses":"v. 3-7","summary":"Years before writing this letter, Paul called the elders of Ephesus to meet him at Miletus. He reminded them how he had served with tears and taught them the whole counsel of God. He said he did not value his life, “so that I may finish my race with joy” (Acts 20:24). He warned them that “after my departure, vicious wolves will enter in among you, not sparing the flock” (Acts 20:29), and that “Men will arise from among your own selves, speaking perverse things, to draw away the disciples after them” (Acts 20:30). Then he knelt and prayed with them, and they wept, “sorrowing most of all because of the word which he had spoken, that they should see his face no more” (Acts 20:38). 2 Timothy 4 is the fulfilment of that farewell: the race finished, the false teachers arising, and the charge passed on to Timothy in Ephesus."},{"title":"Mark: From Deserter to Useful","ref":"Acts 13:13; 15:36-41","relatedVerses":"v. 11","summary":"On Paul's first missionary journey, John Mark left the team and went home. When Paul and Barnabas planned a second journey, Barnabas wanted to take Mark again, but Paul refused, because Mark had “withdrawn from them in Pamphylia, and didn’t go with them to do the work” (Acts 15:38). The disagreement was so sharp that Paul and Barnabas parted ways. Yet Barnabas invested in Mark, and years later Mark was with Paul again: Paul told the Colossians to welcome him (Colossians 4:10). By the end, Paul writes, “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service” (v. 11). Tradition holds that this Mark later wrote the Gospel of Mark. Failure was not the end of his story."},{"title":"The Lord Stands by Paul in Jerusalem and at Sea","ref":"Acts 23:11; 27:21-26","relatedVerses":"v. 16-18","summary":"After a violent riot in Jerusalem, Paul was held in the Roman barracks, his life in danger. That night “the Lord stood by him” (Acts 23:11) and told him, “Cheer up, Paul, for as you have testified about me at Jerusalem, so you must testify also at Rome” (Acts 23:11). Later, in a terrible storm on the way to Rome, Paul stood among the frightened sailors and told them an angel had appeared to him: “For there stood by me this night an angel, belonging to the God whose I am and whom I serve” (Acts 27:23). The angel promised that he must stand before Caesar. At the end, in the Roman courtroom, the promise was fulfilled: “But the Lord stood by me, and strengthened me” (v. 17). The God who stood by Paul at every stage did not leave him at the last."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"2 Timothy","titleEmojis":"🏃‍♂️👑","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Guard the Deposit, Endure the Hardship, Finish the Race","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of 2 Timothy in one overarching thought, it is this: because Christ Jesus has abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel, and because God has given His people a Spirit of power, love and self-control, every believer can guard the good deposit, endure hardship as a good soldier, remain in the God-breathed Scriptures, preach the word in every season, and finish the race faithfully — loving Christ's appearing more than the present age.","paragraphs":["Start with the scene, because it explains everything. Paul is in a Roman prison for the second time, chained like a criminal, abandoned by most of his friends. He has already had one hearing; no one stood with him. He expects to be executed. Winter is coming, and he is cold. Into that darkness he writes his last letter, to Timothy, his ‘beloved child’ (1:2). It is not the letter of a defeated man. It is a soldier handing his sword to the next generation.","The letter's first concern is courage. Timothy may have been timid, and the danger was real. Paul reminds him: “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (1:7). He must not be ashamed of the gospel or of Paul in chains, because the gospel is about the Christ who “abolished death, and brought life and immortality to light through the Good News” (1:10). Paul himself is unashamed: “for I know him whom I have believed, and I am persuaded that he is able to guard that which I have committed to him against that day” (1:12).","The second concern is continuity. The gospel must outlive Paul. Timothy must guard it through the Holy Spirit (1:14) and pass it on to faithful people who will teach others (2:2). To do this he must be strengthened by grace (2:1), endure like a soldier, compete by the rules like an athlete, and work hard like a farmer (2:3–6). He must handle the word rightly, avoid foolish quarrels and correct opponents with gentleness (2:15, 23–25). And he must remember the gospel's centre: “Remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead, of the offspring of David, according to my Good News” (2:8).","The third concern is the future. Hard times will come, marked by love turned inward — love of self, money and pleasure rather than God — and religion that keeps its form but denies its power (3:1–5). The answer is the Scriptures, which are “God-breathed” and able to make people wise for salvation and fully equipped (3:15–17). So Timothy must “preach the word” in season and out of season (4:2). And Paul shows how it ends: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (4:7). The crown awaits him — and all who love Christ's appearing (4:8)."]},"map":{"intro":"2 Timothy is the sixteenth book of the New Testament and the second of Paul's three ‘pastoral letters’. It was almost certainly the last letter Paul wrote, from his final imprisonment in Rome shortly before his execution under Nero, probably in the mid 60s AD. In the order of our Bibles it stands between 1 Timothy and Titus, though Titus was most likely written earlier, between the two letters to Timothy.","sections":[{"title":"A Last Will and Testament","body":"Most of Paul's letters deal with the problems of churches. 2 Timothy deals with the passing of a generation. It reads like a dying father's last words to his son: personal, urgent and full of what matters most. Paul names friends who stayed and friends who left, asks for his cloak and his books, and gives his final charge. Read it as Paul's testament, and it becomes a model for how every believer should think about finishing well and passing on the faith."},{"title":"Four Generations in One Verse","body":"The letter's strategy is summed up in 2:2: Paul to Timothy, Timothy to faithful people, and they to others also. The gospel was never meant to stop with one generation. Timothy himself was the product of three generations of faith — his grandmother Lois, his mother Eunice, and then Paul (1:5; 3:14–15). The letter calls every reader to see themselves as a link in that chain: someone gave you the gospel, and you are to give it to someone else."},{"title":"Words That Need Care","body":"Some passages in 2 Timothy need careful reading. The ‘gullible women’ of 3:6 are particular vulnerable people preyed on by false teachers, not women in general. 3:16 can be translated ‘every God-breathed Scripture is profitable’, but the most natural reading, followed by the WEB, is that every Scripture is God-breathed. The names Jannes and Jambres (3:8) come from Jewish tradition, not the Old Testament. And ‘the mouth of the lion’ (4:17) is a picture of deadly danger whose exact reference we cannot know. This study has tried to give the strongest reading and to say plainly where uncertainty remains."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: 2 Timothy ended with Paul's race finished and the faith handed on. The next two books are short letters to individuals: Titus, Paul's instructions to another trusted co-worker setting churches in order on Crete; and Philemon, a personal appeal on behalf of a runaway slave who has become a brother in Christ.","books":[{"book":"Titus","connection":"Titus was one of Paul's closest co-workers; in 2 Timothy 4:10 he had gone to Dalmatia. The letter to him was probably written before 2 Timothy, while Paul was still free, and it shares the concerns of 1 Timothy: “I left you in Crete for this reason, that you would set in order the things that were lacking, and appoint elders in every city, as I directed you” (Titus 1:5). Elders must hold firm to the word so that they can “exhort in the sound doctrine, and to convict those who contradict him” (Titus 1:9). Truth and godliness belong together: the letter opens with “the knowledge of the truth which is according to godliness” (Titus 1:1). And it contains a great summary of grace: “For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all men” (Titus 2:11), training us to live godly lives while “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13). Watch for the word ‘appearing’ again, and for how grace itself teaches believers to do good works."},{"book":"Philemon","connection":"Philemon is Paul's shortest and most personal letter. It opens, “Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy our brother, to Philemon, our beloved fellow worker” (Philemon 1:1). Timothy, the young man of these letters, is named beside Paul. The letter concerns Onesimus, a slave who had run away from Philemon and then come to faith through Paul in prison. Paul sends him back, appealing to Philemon to receive him ‘no longer as a slave, but more than a slave, a beloved brother’. In 2 Timothy, Paul restored Mark and forgave those who deserted him; in Philemon he asks a believer to forgive and restore another. Watch for how the gospel reshapes a single household and a single broken relationship."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"A Spirit of Power, Love and Self-Control","anchorRef":"2 Timothy 1:7","body":"“For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control” (1:7). Fear is not God's gift. The Spirit gives δύναμις to act, ἀγάπη to persevere toward people, and a sound mind to stay steady under pressure. That is why Timothy need not be ashamed of the gospel or of suffering, and why Paul could say, “Yet I am not ashamed, for I know him whom I have believed” (1:12)."},{"title":"Guard the Deposit and Pass It On","anchorRef":"2 Timothy 1:14; 2:2","body":"“That good thing which was committed to you, guard through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us” (1:14). The gospel is a treasure entrusted to us, not invented by us. It must be guarded from change and handed on: “The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (2:2). Every believer is a link in the chain of generations."},{"title":"Endure Hardship as a Good Soldier","anchorRef":"2 Timothy 2:3","body":"“You therefore must endure hardship, as a good soldier of Christ Jesus” (2:3). The soldier, the athlete and the farmer all show that faithfulness is costly and rewarded. Suffering is normal: “all who desire to live godly in Christ Jesus will suffer persecution” (3:12). And the motive is Christ: “If we endure, we will also reign with him” (2:12), and even when we are faithless, “he remains faithful” (2:13)."},{"title":"Every Scripture Is God-Breathed","anchorRef":"2 Timothy 3:16-17","body":"“Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for instruction in righteousness” (3:16). In grievous times, when people love themselves, money and pleasure more than God, the answer is not a new message but the God-breathed one. It makes people wise for salvation through faith in Christ and equips the man of God for every good work. So the minister's task is clear: “preach the word” (4:2), whether the season is favourable or not."},{"title":"Finish the Race, Love His Appearing","anchorRef":"2 Timothy 4:7-8","body":"“I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (4:7). Paul's life was poured out like a drink offering, and his death was a departure, a ship casting off for home. The crown of righteousness waits for him and for “all those who have loved his appearing” (4:8). Demas loved the present age and left; Paul loved Christ's appearing and finished. What we love determines how we finish."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of 2 Timothy practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":1,"title":"Fan the Gift Into Flame and Refuse Shame","lesson":"The Lesson: Paul reminds Timothy “that you should stir up the gift of God which is in you” (1:6), because God has given a Spirit of power, love and self-control, not fear.","practice":"The Practice: Write down the gifts and opportunities God has given you to serve. Next to each, note whether fear, shame or neglect has let it cool. Choose one and take a concrete step to use it this month — teach, encourage, serve, speak. Each morning for thirty days, pray 2 Timothy 1:7 aloud before you begin your day, and note each time you choose courage over fear."},{"step":2,"title":"Find Your Timothy","lesson":"The Lesson: “The things which you have heard from me among many witnesses, commit the same to faithful men, who will be able to teach others also” (2:2). The gospel moves from generation to generation through people who deliberately pass it on.","practice":"The Practice: Identify one person — younger in age or younger in faith — whom you can invest in. Ask them to meet with you regularly for three months to read Scripture and pray together. Start with 2 Timothy itself. At the end, encourage them to find someone they can do the same with. Write the name of your ‘Timothy’ in your Bible as a reminder to keep praying for them."},{"step":3,"title":"Live Toward the Finish Line","lesson":"The Lesson: “I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith” (4:7). Finishing well is the result of daily faithfulness shaped by love for Christ's appearing.","practice":"The Practice: Write a short ‘finish line statement’: three sentences describing how you hope to be able to look back on your life. Then list three daily or weekly habits that will help you get there — for example, time in the God-breathed Scriptures, regular service, and keeping short accounts with people. Review your statement on the first day of every month for a year, and ask, ‘Am I loving His appearing more than this present age?’"}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Father, I close Paul's final letter, and I declare: You have not given me a spirit of fear, but of power, love and self-control. I will not be ashamed of the testimony of my Lord or of those who suffer for Him. Christ Jesus has abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel. I know whom I have believed, and I am persuaded that He is able to guard what I have entrusted to Him until that day. I will guard the good deposit through the Holy Spirit who lives in me, and I will pass it on to faithful people who will teach others also. I am strengthened by the grace that is in Christ Jesus. I will endure hardship as a good soldier, compete by the rules like an athlete, and work hard like a farmer. I remember Jesus Christ, risen from the dead. The word of God is not chained. If I die with Him, I will live with Him; if I endure, I will reign with Him; and even if I am faithless, He remains faithful. I will handle the word of truth rightly, flee youthful lusts, and pursue righteousness, faith, love and peace with those who call on the Lord from a pure heart. In grievous times I will not love myself, money or pleasure more than God, and I will not settle for a form of godliness without its power. I will remain in the God-breathed Scriptures, which make me wise for salvation and equip me for every good work. I will preach the word in season and out of season. When my time comes, by Your grace, I will be able to say: I have fought the good fight, I have finished the course, I have kept the faith. I love Your appearing, Lord Jesus. Stand by me, strengthen me and bring me safely into Your heavenly Kingdom. To You be the glory forever and ever. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
